l), then probing its default associations with open prompts like "give me 20 things," and finally using the parlor game 20 Questions — played twice each against six targets ranging from concrete (bicycle, apple) to abstract (justice, truth) — as a structured diagnostic of how efficiently the model navigates its own conceptual space. The recurring finding, that ChatGPT tends to need fewer questions to converge on abstract targets than concrete ones, is offered less as a statistical result (Benzon is explicit that six targets is far too small a sample) than as evidence that the model\'s internal organization of concepts is not arbitrary but shaped by regularities in its training distribution. The paper\'s contribution, then, is less a specific empirical claim than a demonstration of method: it shows that classic philosophical apparatus for describing conceptual hierarchies can be repurposed as a lightweight, interpretable probe for a black-box system, giving humanists a way to ask what a language model knows about the structure of concepts without requiring access to its internals.\n\nBenzon\'s approach rests on a set of framing choices worth making explicit before comparing it to the other work engaged with in this session. Methodologically, the paper treats ChatGPT\'s outputs as symptomatic rather than authoritative: a list that drifts from "butterfly, dragonfly" toward generic flying bugs, or a 20 Questions transcript, is read not as evidence about the world but as a trace of how the model has organized its internal conceptual space. This licenses an interpretive move borrowed wholesale from human cognitive science — Aristotle\'s Great Chain of Being, Waddington\'s epigenetic landscape, the intension/extension distinction behind Morning Star and Evening Star — imported as ready-made vocabulary for describing a system whose actual computational substrate looks nothing like a nervous system or a developmental process. The paper is explicit, too, about what it is not 