g 29, Listening 30, Speaking 23, Writing 24) \\u2014 10/15/2025", "institution": "Goethe University Frankfurt", "location": "Frankfurt, Germany", "start_time": "04/2024"}}], "interests": {".gitkeep": "", "_.md": ""}, "languages": [{"entry": {"language": "Chinese", "proficiency": "C2"}}, {"entry": {"language": "German", "proficiency": "C1"}}, {"entry": {"language": "English", "proficiency": "C2"}}, {"entry": {"language": "Japanese", "proficiency": "A2"}}], "publications": [{"entry": {"authors": "An Qi", "date": "2021", "title": "Design of a Skin Cancer Diagnosing Web Application Based on Convolutional Neural Network Model and Chatterbot Application Programming Interface", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1088/1742-6596/2078/1/012039", "venue": "2021 J. Phys.: Conf. Ser. 2078 012039"}}], "research_experience": [{"entry": {"advisor": "", "description": "Bachelor thesis, \\"An Ontological Perspective on LLM Beliefs\\" -- ongoing.", "end_time": "", "institution": "Goethe University Frankfurt", "lab": "", "role": "Bachelor Thesis Student", "start_time": ""}}, {"entry": {"advisor": "", "description": "Lambda Calculus (categorical formalization). Took a categorical approach to the mathematical formalization of the Lambda calculus, using a framework similar to the de Bruijn index: constructed a language-like structure with axioms mimicking classical beta-calculus, using abstract mathematical objects as variables to avoid naming collisions, and proved several propositions about the constructed language. Delivered a rigorous categorical definition of the Lambda calculus, an instance defined over natural numbers, and a Python interpreter for it mimicking the de Bruijn index.", "end_time": "10/2025", "institution": "", "lab": "", "role": "Independent Researcher", "start_time": "10/2025"}}, {"entry": {"advisor": "", "description": "Hough Transform (continuous generalization). Proposed and formalized a continuous counterpart to the classical (discrete) Hough transform for line detection, drawing an analogy to the Fourier transform; mathematically proved the continuous version linear and invertible, then used discrete, realistic images to approximate the ideal continuous case in practice.", "end_time": "04/2023", "institution": "", "lab": "", "role": "Independent Researcher", "start_time": "04/2023"}}], "skills": {".gitkeep": "", "_.md": "- **Programming:** Python, Java, Haskell, Lean4, C#, Matlab\\n- **Languages:** Chinese (C2), German (C1), English (C2), Japanese (A2)"}, "working_experience": {".gitkeep": "", "entry": {".gitkeep": "", "company": "", "description": "", "end_time": "", "location": "", "role": "", "start_time": ""}}}, "summary": {".gitkeep": "", "_.md": "Bachelor of Science in Computer Science student at Goethe University Frankfurt, working on an ontological perspective on LLM beliefs for his bachelor thesis. Experience spans deep learning and computer vision (neuroscience-inspired image generation pipelines), LLM-driven software synthesis tooling, categorical/mathematical formalization of the Lambda calculus, and earlier work in medical image classification and game development."}}\n\nProjects JSON:\n[{".entry": {}, "info": {"context": "Bachelorarbeit at Goethe-Universit\\u00e4t Frankfurt am Main, Fachbereich Informatik und Mathematik, first examiner Prof. Dr. Visvanathan Ramesh. Thesis reproduces a mechanistic-interpretability protocol on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct separating answer generation from observation of verbal self-reports (confidence), and extends it to a family of verbal self-reports. Keywords: large language models; confidence; mechanistic interpretability; calibration; ontology; activation steering.", "end_time": "31 August 2026", "role": "Author (Bachelor\'s thesis student, B.Sc. Informatik)", "start_time": "27 June 2026", "titile": "An Ontological Perspective on LLM Beliefs"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# An Ontological Perspective on LLM Beliefs\\n\\nA Bachelor\'s thesis (B.Sc. Informatik, Goethe-Universit\\u00e4t Frankfurt) by Qi An, examined by Prof. Dr. Visvanathan Ramesh, submitted 31 August 2026. The thesis investigates whether large language models\' verbal self-reports (e.g., confidence) reflect information cached near the generated answer and later retrieved, or are computed on the fly at the moment they are elicited \\u2014 and whether this generalizes beyond confidence to other kinds of self-report.\\n\\n**Reproduction.** The work first reproduces, at reduced scale on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, the mechanistic protocol of Kumaran et al. (2026), which separates an answer-generation phase (Phase 0) from a self-report observation phase (Phase 1) and reads a confidence class from logits at a final cue. Using activation-steering interventions at the answer-adjacent position (PANL) versus the final classification cue (CC) and positional controls, the reduced run finds the clearest confidence change occurs when intervening directly at CC, with little consistent effect at PANL \\u2014 meaning it does not reproduce the anchor study\'s proposed answer-adjacent/cached-retrieval dissociation.\\n\\n**Extension: vonto.** The thesis then introduces vonto, a framework generalizing the protocol from confidence to four additional self-report constructs \\u2014 commitment, nuance, challenge, variety, and impurity \\u2014 each paired with an independently computed ground truth (correctness, answer-sequence probability, answer entropy, challenge susceptibility, list variance, and Gini impurity respectively), deliberately keeping the observation method distinct from the ground truth. This is evaluated across four dataset families: TriviaQA, a lexical-relations (synonym/co-hyponym) dataset, List Elicitation, and Twenty Questions.\\n\\n**Results.** Calibration (Spearman rank correlation) is reported for all four datasets: lexical-relation reports are dominated by binary correctness, whereas TriviaQA commitment and nuance align more strongly with answer-sequence probability and entropy. List variety and twenty-questions impurity show modest positive associations with their intended measures, though variety is similarly correlated with a hidden temperature control, undermining construct specificity. Challenge results are inconsistent in direction across datasets. Completed causal steering analyses (bidirectional, layer-swept activation steering) are limited to TriviaQA confidence and Synonyms commitment; both show strong effects only at the final readout cue, not at answer-adjacent positions, and the Twenty Questions sample produced constant impurity reports, precluding a steering contrast.\\n\\n**Interpretation.** The evidence supports only a limited conclusion: self-reports can be causally altered at their final readout cue, but this doesn\'t distinguish cached retrieval from just-in-time computation, and the narrow scope restricts any broader claim. Drawing on Benzon\'s ontological vocabulary (inheritance structures, paradigmatic/ontological transitions) as an interpretive lens rather than an empirical theory, the thesis favors an \\"observer-relative\\" reading of LLM belief \\u2014 reports are prompted, measured artifacts dependent on wording, tokenizer, and readout procedure \\u2014 while leaving representational-state and linguistic-performance interpretations open. \\"Belief\\" is explicitly used operationally, not as a claim of human-like propositional attitudes. Limitations include single-model/single-dataset scope, small held-out samples, unadjusted multiple comparisons, and imperfect ground-truth measures (e.g., alias-matching correctness). Future work calls for larger preregistered steering studies, cross-architecture tests (e.g., Gemma), and adversarial/alternative ground-truth probes.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "A pipeline for Deep Learning for Computer Vision connecting neuroscience-derived image transformations with Vision-Language Model analysis, built on the BrainACTIV framework to predict and manipulate fMRI cortical activations; implemented a Python Imset/Roit/Roid pipeline using SLERP-modulated CLIP embeddings fed into an IP-Adapter-conditioned Stable Diffusion img2img model, plus VLM analysis of 9,865 image embeddings across ten ROIs.", "end_time": "04/2026", "role": "Coursework project developer", "start_time": "04/2026", "titile": "BrainACTIV-VLM"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# BrainACTIV-VLM Analysis\\n\\n**BrainACTIV-VLM Analysis** (Qi An, Abd Alselam Ahmad; April 2026) documents a two-part pipeline connecting neuroscience-derived image transformations to vision-language model (VLM) analysis, investigating what visual concepts drive activation in specific brain regions when translated into CLIP semantic space.\\n\\n## Part 1: Python-ROI Pipeline\\n\\nThe pipeline transforms natural images into region-of-interest (ROI)-specific variants using BrainACTIV-derived embeddings and a Stable Diffusion v1.5 img2img model conditioned via IP-Adapter (no text prompt required). It targets ten cortical ROIs: FFA (faces), EBA (body parts), VWFA (text), OPA (objects/spatial layout), PPA (places), RSC (navigation-relevant scenes), and early visual areas V1-V4.\\n\\nFor each image, a reference CLIP embedding is spherically interpolated (SLERP) toward a precomputed, ROI-specific \\"mode embedding\\" that encodes the region\'s preferred direction in CLIP space, with a falling back to linear interpolation when vectors are nearly collinear. The modulated embedding conditions the diffusion model\'s cross-attention to generate a new image maximizing predicted activation in the target ROI.\\n\\nThree cooperating Python modules implement this: **Imset**, a recursive dictionary-based image-dataset abstraction that loads directory trees of images (with a singleton cache to avoid redundant loads); **Roit**, the transformation engine implementing the CLIP-extraction/SLERP/diffusion steps; and **Roid**, an orchestrator that applies all ten ROI transforms across a dataset. The pipeline runs via a `roid` CLI entry point and is deployable as a SLURM GPU job for large-scale batch processing.\\n\\n## Part 2: VLM Semantic Analysis\\n\\nThe second part classifies the transformed embeddings against a fixed semantic taxonomy using cosine similarity, applied to 9,865 image embeddings across all ten ROIs from a `tiny-test-10k-emb` dataset. A `CosineClassifier` encodes text labels via CLIP\'s text encoder, computes cosine similarity against image embeddings, aggregates scores by category, and assigns the highest-scoring category.\\n\\nTwo analyses are run: **validation**, using a narrow functionally-aligned label set (faces, body parts, text, objects, places, spatial context) to build 6\\u00d76 confusion heatmaps testing whether each ROI\'s modulation steers toward its expected category; and **experiment**, using a broader 20-category taxonomy (animals, clothing, furniture, tools, buildings, etc.) to build 20\\u00d720 remapping heatmaps and reference/modulated/difference distribution plots.\\n\\n## Key Findings\\n\\nModulation strongly reshapes semantic classification, often diverging from the ROIs\' classical neuroscientific labels. EBA and VWFA show the most extreme, near-total remapping \\u2014 nearly all images shift to \\"sports\\" (99%) and \\"tools\\" (95%) respectively. FFA modulation concentrates mass in \\"body parts\\" and \\"occupations,\\" consistent with its face-selectivity. Early visual areas (V1-V4) show broader, less intuitive attractors: V1 toward food/furniture, V2-V3 toward furniture/tools/buildings, and V4 toward art/stationery. Spatial areas (OPA, PPA, RSC) concentrate toward furniture, animals, and buildings respectively, loosely aligned with scene-related concepts. The \\"difference panel\\" analysis (classifying vmod \\u2212 vref directly) isolates each ROI\'s raw CLIP-space deviation direction independent of source content, confirming these same semantic attractors. The authors conclude the work establishes a quantitative bridge between fMRI-derived ROI models and vision-language semantics, while showing that CLIP-space deviation directions don\'t always map cleanly onto the ROIs\' textbook functional roles \\u2014 a gap the authors treat as informative about how neural preferences are encoded in language-vision representations.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "A Software System Engineering project: a CLI pipeline and web-based IDE that automates stage-by-stage software project generation using LLMs, translating requirements into formal artifacts across Git-versioned stages; includes a React+Vite/FastAPI web IDE with Monaco editor, live WebSocket console, and a Visualizer Pane using a secondary Mistral LLM call, covered by 84 automated tests.", "end_time": "04/2026", "role": "Coursework project developer", "start_time": "04/2026", "titile": "LIPS: LLM-driven IterPatternative Project Synthesis"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# LIPS & LIPSIDE \\u2014 LLM-Driven Iterative Project Synthesis\\n\\nThis project source contains two companion Python packages: **LIPS**, a pipeline engine, and **LIPSIDE**, a web-based IDE built on top of it.\\n\\n## LIPS\\n\\nLIPS is a framework for orchestrating multi-stage LLM transformation pipelines. Each **pipeline** is an ordered set of **stages**, and each stage transforms a **source repository** into a **target repository** using a **build file** (Markdown, Python, or Shell). Markdown build files are the primary mechanism: they are natural-language prompts sent to an LLM, augmented with `env` blocks (injected environment variables), `sourceignore`/`targetignore` blocks (gitignore-style filtering), and `[write:virtual/path](./real/path)` links that embed file or directory contents \\u2014 including automatically extracted text from PDFs, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint files \\u2014 as `<file>` blocks in the prompt. The LLM\'s response is parsed for `<file path=\\"...\\">content</file>` tags, and those files are written into the target stage\'s `repo/` directory (empty content deletes a file).\\n\\nConfiguration lives in a workspace-level `config.json`, specifying the LLM model (via LiteLLM\'s `\\"provider/model\\"` string, supporting 40+ providers such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral, Google, Bedrock, and local Ollama/vLLM), generation parameters (max tokens, temperature, timeout), the API key environment variable, few-shot `messages`, and an informational pipeline graph. API keys are supplied via `.env` files.\\n\\nThe CLI (`lips`) provides three commands: `lips create` (interactive wizard to scaffold a pipeline\'s directories and config), `lips build [build_file] <stage_path>` (executes a stage\'s build file and calls the LLM or script), and `lips purge` (clears generated `repo/` contents and `out/` logs while preserving directory structure and `.gitignore`). Every build run is logged to a timestamped `out/<provider>/<masked_api_key>/<model>/<timestamp>/` directory containing `messages.json`, `response.md`, and `files.json` for full auditability and reproducibility, with local file paths masked in logs. The codebase is organized under `lips/lips/` into `api/` (core generation logic), `commands/` (CLI subcommands), and `utils/` (file parsing, build-file parsing, and Markdown link/env resolution helpers).\\n\\n## LIPSIDE\\n\\nLIPSIDE is a FastAPI + vanilla-JavaScript web application that provides a VS Code\\u2013inspired browser IDE for managing LIPS workspaces without touching the command line. Its five-row layout (menu bar, title bar, sidebar, editor, preview, build status/output panels) lets users browse pipeline stages via sidebar views (`repo`, `build`, `out`, and a Mermaid-based `graph` view), edit build files with CodeMirror-based multi-tab syntax highlighting and Markdown preview, and manage pipeline configuration (model, tokens, temperature, API keys, few-shot message templates) and `.env` files at workspace/pipeline/stage scope through dialogs rather than raw file edits.\\n\\nBuilds are triggered via a \\"Run Build\\" action that streams live output over WebSocket (`/ws/build`), and an embedded PTY-backed terminal (`/ws/terminal`, using native `pty` on Unix and `pywinpty` on Windows) offers direct shell access, alongside the option to launch a native OS terminal window. The backend is split into seven router modules (`workspace`, `files`, `build`, `create`, `config`, `purge`, `terminal`) exposing a REST + WebSocket API for workspace/pipeline/stage/file operations, all guarded by path-traversal and symlink-safety checks, with deletions routed through the system recycle bin. The server binds to `127.0.0.1` by default and can optionally be exposed on all interfaces.\\n\\nTogether, the two projects form a full stack for auditable, multi-provider, LLM-driven code/document transformation: LIPS as the reproducible pipeline execution engine, and LIPSIDE as its visual authoring and monitoring environment.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "Interdisciplinary practical project at Goethe University Frankfurt advising a fictional university senate commission on extreme weather risks to campus operations, focused on prolonged heat and shading measures; conducted surveys and temperature measurements, developed pergola/pavilion shading prototypes, and presented findings in a report and final presentation.", "end_time": "07/2026", "role": "Team member (8-person cross-year team)", "start_time": "", "titile": "Heat Adaptations on Campus Riedberg"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# HARI \\u2013 Hitzeanpassungen am Campus Riedberg\\n\\nThis is the final report of **Team 2 (\\"Die Wettermagneten\\")**, eight students in the Goethe-Universit\\u00e4t Frankfurt \\"Praxisprojekt Extremwetter\\" (Summer 2026), submitted 06.07.2026. The project investigates heat stress (\\"Hitze\\") as an extreme-weather risk on the university\'s Campus Riedberg, focusing specifically on the underused, largely unshaded green spaces between the Biozentrum and the Otto-Stern-Zentrum (OSZ) \\u2014 referred to in the report as \\"OSZ Gr\\u00fcn\\" and \\"Bio Wiese.\\"\\n\\n**Method.** The team combined objective and subjective data collection. Infrared surface-temperature measurements were taken on two hot days (23 and 25 May 2026, 14:00\\u201316:00) across various ground materials (concrete pavers, natural stone, cobblestone, gravel, lawn, wood) in both sun and shade, with GPS-tagged, repeated readings at each spot. A supplementary experiment measured how ground temperature decays with distance from a sunlit edge into shade, yielding a linear regression (T = -0.0951\\u00b7d + 48.81, R\\u00b2 = 0.982) showing roughly 1\\u00b0C cooling per 10 cm toward shade. A parallel online survey (Google Forms, 9\\u201330 June 2026, n=24, mostly students) assessed satisfaction with the space and desired improvements, with open-text answers manually coded into thematic categories and organized into a normalized relational data model (documented in the appendix).\\n\\n**Findings.** Sealed/paved surfaces in direct sun reached 50\\u201352\\u00b0C, about 20\\u00b0C above ambient air temperature (31\\u201332\\u00b0C). Shading the same surface types cooled them by up to ~21.6\\u00b0C. Dried-out lawn areas measured 37\\u201340\\u00b0C, intermediate between paved and shaded surfaces \\u2014 indicating that without irrigation, green space loses much of its cooling benefit. Survey respondents rated satisfaction with the space a middling 5/10; 63% rarely or never use the existing seating, and 58% said they would definitely use the space more if tables were available. The dominant complaints were lack of shade, greenery, and seating/tables.\\n\\n**Recommendation.** Because shading is the most effective and fastest-to-implement lever (versus full de-sealing, which is complicated by the architect\'s copyright over the building\'s surrounding design and high excavation costs), the team recommends a pilot installation of two pergolas and two pavilions on the lawn in front of the Otto-Stern-Zentrum, estimated at \\u20ac15,000\\u201320,000 for green, table-equipped units, complemented by long-term tree planting. They cite the \\"VERD\\u00b0-UNIT\\" by Frankfurt firm OMC\\u00b0C \\u2014 a freestanding, plant-covered shade module already piloted since 2023 at the Senckenberg Naturmuseum \\u2014 as a concrete precedent worth considering for this pilot. Misting systems were explicitly ruled out due to wind exposure and running costs.\\n\\nThe report includes full raw data appendices: individual infrared measurement series (cobblestone terrace, n=11 each in sun/shade; OSZ lawn, n=31) and the complete anonymized survey dataset structured as lookup tables (study program, degree, semester, gender, usage frequency, table preference, question fields, response categories) linked to a core \\"Befragte\\" (respondents) table and a folded free-text response table.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "Personal research taking a categorical approach to formalizing the Lambda calculus using a de Bruijn Index-like framework with abstract mathematical objects as variables; includes a rigorous categorical definition, an instance over natural numbers, and a Python interpreter.", "end_time": "10/2025", "role": "Independent researcher", "start_time": "10/2025", "titile": "Lambda Calculus (categorical formalization)"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# Complete Lambda Calculus\\n\\nThis project (a Jupyter/Colab notebook, `Complete_Lambda_Calculus.ipynb`) develops an original, rigorously axiomatized formulation of lambda calculus from first principles, then implements it as a working Python interpreter.\\n\\n## Theoretical foundation\\n\\nThe notebook opens with set-theoretic preliminaries \\u2014 tuple mapping of functions, image/preimage operators, successor and binary minimum \\u2014 before defining a **lambda calculus** as a tuple $(L_{\\\\geq0}, V, \\\\lambda, \\\\mu)$: a set $L$ equipped with an abstraction operator $\\\\lambda$, an application operator $\\\\mu$ (written as a dot product), a \\"degree of negligence\\" function $\\\\delta$ (essentially a de Bruijn-index-like binding depth), and a countable set of variables $V$. The axioms require that a customized substitution operator $\\\\beta_{v_k\\\\to x_0}$ and the depth function $\\\\delta$ each be well-defined (single-valued) via auxiliary \\"imaged value\\" set operators $B$ and $D$, and that application/abstraction interact through the substitution law $\\\\mu(\\\\lambda(y), x_0) = \\\\beta_{v_1\\\\to x_0}(y)$. A proposition then shows substitution is *not* uniquely defined in general (demonstrated with a concrete counterexample $x=v_1$, $y=\\\\lambda(v_2)v_1$), motivating the restriction to $L_{\\\\geq0}$.\\n\\nBuilding on this, the notebook defines **computability**: a function is computable if it equals $F_y(x) = y\\\\cdot x$ for some $y \\\\in L_{\\\\geq0}$, gives the expression $Y_f$ of a computable function, and proves the halting-problem-style result that the \\"inverse operator\\" (mapping invertible $y$ to the expression of $F_y^{-1}$) is not computable. It then instantiates the abstract theory concretely as lambda calculus over $\\\\mathbb{N}$, defining terms $\\\\Lambda$, an equivalence relation via $\\\\beta^*$-reduction, and a recursive definition of the reduction function itself.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\nThe code section implements this as a small interpreter:\\n- An `Expression` base class with subclasses `Variable`, `Abstraction`, `Application`, `Name`, and `Church` (numerals), supporting `beta` substitution, `neg` (binding-depth check), and a depth-first `dfs_reduce`/`eval`/`dfs_derive` evaluator that prints each reduction step.\\n- A `Lark`-based LALR parser with custom grammar supporting De Bruijn-style variables (`v1`, `v2`, ...), `L`/`\\u03bb` abstraction (with `L^n` shorthand for repeated abstraction), Church numeral literals, named terms, and juxtaposition or dot-separated application.\\n- A library of named combinators built up from the primitives: Boolean algebra (`true`, `false`, `if`, `and`, `or`, `not`), data structures (`pair`, `first`/`second`, `nil`/`null`, `cons`), Church-numeral arithmetic (`succ`, `pred`, `add`, `mul`, `sub`, `div`, `gcd`, `leq`, `equal`) via a fixed-point combinator `fix`, rational-number arithmetic (`Q`, `Qadd`), and a generated family of fixed-length vector/tuple constructors and projections (`vecN`, `takeNofK`).\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nA worked derivation trace demonstrates `take3of3` correctly projecting from a 3-vector through many beta-reduction steps. The final cells show an unfinished experiment (`take3of3 ()`) that raises a parser error, indicating the notebook is a work in progress rather than a finished, polished artifact \\u2014 closer to research/exploratory scratch work formalizing lambda calculus mathematically and validating the implementation interactively.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "Research proposing a continuous analog of the classical Hough Transform (drawing an analogy to the Fourier transform), formalizing and proving it linear and invertible, then approximating it with discrete realistic images.", "end_time": "04/2023", "role": "Independent researcher", "start_time": "04/2023", "titile": "Hough Transform (continuous generalization)"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# Hough Transform \\u2014 Project Summary\\n\\nThis project, authored by Qi An (July 2020), is a theoretical paper that reformulates the classical Hough transform in continuous mathematical terms, aiming to prove that a well-designed version of the transform is invertible and therefore information-preserving \\u2014 a property relevant to embedding learning in machine learning contexts.\\n\\n## Background and Motivation\\n\\nThe Hough transform, originally patented by Paul Hough in 1962 and later generalized by Dana Ballard in 1981 to detect arbitrary parametric shapes, is a longstanding feature-extraction technique in image analysis. Prior work on its invertibility (notably by Anastasios Kesidis and collaborators) relied on discrete formulations, which necessarily lose information because the input image must first undergo edge detection (e.g., Canny). The author\'s central motivation is to avoid this information loss by defining the Hough transform first in a fully continuous (\\"infinite\\") setting, and only then discretizing it, so that invertibility properties can be established more rigorously.\\n\\n## Core Contributions\\n\\n1. **Infinite \\u03c8-Hough Accumulator and Transform**: The paper defines a general Hough accumulator `h_\\u03c8` parameterized by an injective mapping \\u03c8 from a d-dimensional parameter space to subsets of \\u211d\\u00b2. It proves a key duality lemma (Lemma 1.1) showing that membership in the accumulator\'s preimage set \\u0393_\\u03c8(x,y) is equivalent to membership of (x,y) in \\u03c8(u). This leads to a clean interpretation: the infinite Hough transform of an image \\u03c6 at parameter u is simply the integral of \\u03c6 over the shape \\u03c8(u) \\u2014 i.e., it measures how much \\"mass\\" or intensity lies along that shape.\\n\\n2. **Concrete Examples**: The framework is instantiated for the classic line detection case (parameters \\u03b8, r) and for circle detection (parameters a, b, r, or a, b with fixed radius R), showing the general definitions recover familiar Hough transform variants.\\n\\n3. **Finite Hough Transform**: The paper then discretizes the infinite construction into matrices, defining accumulator matrices and a finite Hough transform on an m\\u2081\\u00d7m\\u2082 image producing an n\\u2081\\u00d7n\\u2082\\u00d7...\\u00d7n_d output array, using floor-based discretization of the continuous accumulator.\\n\\n4. **Pseudo-Inverse (Theorem 10)**: The main technical result reshapes the image and accumulator into vectors/matrices and expresses the finite Hough transform as a linear map J = H\\u00b7I. When the output dimensionality is large enough (Qd ni \\u2265 input size), a pseudo-inverse H_inv can approximately recover the original image I from its Hough transform J. This is presented as evidence that the finite Hough transform, properly sized, is approximately invertible and thus preserves information \\u2014 motivating its potential use as a feature/embedding layer in learning systems.\\n\\n## Notable Characteristics\\n\\nThe document is a compact (4-page) theoretical note with LaTeX-typeset definitions, lemmas, and proofs, plus a placeholder \\"Related works\\" section listing topic headers without full citations. The prose contains occasional typos/informal phrasing (e.g., \\"weather,\\" \\"Canney,\\" \\"reserves\\" instead of \\"preserves\\") consistent with an early draft or personal research note rather than a polished, peer-reviewed publication. The single reference is Hough\'s original 1962 U.S. patent.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "A web application for skin cancer diagnosis combining a CNN model (0.95 classification accuracy) with a Chatterbot-API-driven chatbot answering 100+ questions; published as a peer-reviewed conference paper (J. Phys.: Conf. Ser. 2078 012039).", "end_time": "01/2021", "role": "Developer/Author", "start_time": "01/2021", "titile": "Skin Cancer Diagnosing Web Application"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# Skin Cancer Diagnosing Web Application (CNN + Chatterbot)\\n\\n**Source:** Qi An, \\"Design of a Skin Cancer Diagnosing Web Application Based on Convolutional Neural Network Model and Chatterbot Application Programming Interface,\\" *J. Phys.: Conf. Ser.* 2078 012039 (2021), ICAITA 2021, Institute for Mathematics, Technical University of Berlin.\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThis project presents a full-stack web application that helps users self-assess suspicious skin lesions by combining a convolutional neural network (CNN) image classifier with a Chatterbot-based conversational assistant. The motivation is that malignant melanoma, the deadliest skin cancer, is highly treatable when caught early, yet existing consumer tools (FirstCheck, UMSkinCheck, SkinVision) either lack instant machine-learning-based feedback, require manual self-study, or are paywalled. The author\'s goal was to build a free, ML-driven, interactive alternative.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n\\nThe application follows a frontend/backend split:\\n\\n- **Frontend:** Built with Vue.js. Users upload a photo of a skin lesion and can type free-text questions about skin cancer into a chat interface.\\n- **Backend:** Two deep-learning-adjacent components\\u2014\\n  1. **CNN Model:** Classifies the uploaded image into one of 7 diagnostic skin-lesion categories. The architecture stacks four 2D convolutional layers (with max-pooling after the first two), a flatten layer (12,544 units), and four dense layers (128 \\u2192 64 \\u2192 32 \\u2192 7), with the final layer producing a 7-dimensional probability vector.\\n  2. **Chatbot:** A Python NLP model built on the Chatterbot API, trained via a \\"listing trainer\\" on 270 question-answer pairs about skin cancer scraped from Quora using Python\'s GoogleSearch and BeautifulSoup libraries. It answers user questions by finding the closest matching crawled question and returning its associated answer, informed by the CNN\'s prediction.\\n\\n## Data and Training\\n\\nThe CNN was trained on the HAM10000 (\\"Human Against Machine with 10000 training images\\") dataset\\u201410,015 dermatoscopic images across 7 diagnostic categories, using the pre-vectorized `hmnist_28_28_L.csv` (28\\u00d728 grayscale) plus `HAM10000_metadata.csv` for labels/demographics. The data was split 7,511/2,504 (train/test), class-balanced via SMOTE (expanding the training set to 35,322 examples), and trained for 50 epochs (batch size 256, learning rate 1e-3) on Kaggle, using sigmoid/softmax output activations and binary cross-entropy loss with backpropagation.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nThe CNN achieved ~0.99 training accuracy and converged to ~0.85 accuracy on the held-out test set after about 20 epochs (the abstract separately cites 0.95 for a binary/simpler classification framing). The chatbot could answer 100+ skin-cancer-related questions.\\n\\n## Limitations and Future Work\\n\\nThe author notes this is a \\"basic\\" prototype suitable for non-professional/informational use rather than clinical diagnosis. Proposed extensions include: real-time camera-based capture, cross-user data aggregation for comparative diagnosis, upgrading the chatbot to abstractive (rather than extractive/rule-based) summarization, more advanced oversampling to address class imbalance and overfitting, and extending the tool from a patient-facing app into a patient-to-dermatologist support system that supplies AI-generated diagnostic reports to clinicians.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "A personal Python flashcard app with AI-driven card generation from single words and paragraph analysis that extracts unfamiliar words and auto-generates flashcards.", "end_time": "10/2024", "role": "Developer", "start_time": "10/2024", "titile": "Flashcard Python Application"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# Flashcard Python Application\\n\\n**Role:** Developer\\n**Timeline:** October 2024\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nA personal flashcard application built in Python, designed to streamline vocabulary acquisition through AI-assisted content generation. Rather than requiring manual entry for every card, the app leverages AI to automatically generate flashcards from minimal input, reducing the friction typically associated with building a study deck.\\n\\n## Key Features\\n\\n- **Single-word card generation:** Users can input an individual word, and the AI generates a complete flashcard, likely including definitions, usage context, and related information, without requiring the user to write the content themselves.\\n- **Paragraph analysis:** The application can take a block of text as input, analyze it to identify unfamiliar or advanced vocabulary, and automatically extract those words to generate flashcards for them. This feature is particularly useful for turning passive reading material (articles, books, etc.) into active study material.\\n- **AI-driven content generation:** Both core features rely on AI to do the heavy lifting of content creation, positioning the tool as an intelligent study aid rather than a simple flashcard storage system.\\n\\n## Purpose and Value\\n\\nThe project addresses a common friction point in vocabulary learning: the tedium of manually creating flashcards. By automating both single-word lookups and full-paragraph vocabulary extraction, the app enables a more natural learning workflow, allowing users to convert everyday reading into structured study material with minimal manual effort.\\n\\n## Technical Notes\\n\\nBuilt entirely in Python within a short, focused development window (October 2024), suggesting a lightweight, personal-use tool rather than a large-scale production system. The reliance on AI for content generation implies integration with a language model (likely via an API) to handle the natural language understanding tasks of word analysis and flashcard content creation.\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nDevelopment occurred over a single month (October 2024), indicating this was likely a short, self-contained project or an initial build phase. No further details are available regarding ongoing maintenance or feature expansion beyond this period.\\n"}}, {"info": {"context": "A recreation of 5D Chess with Multiverse Time Travel built in Unity 3D and C#, using a 3D visualization approach for the chessboard instead of the original\'s 2D representation.", "end_time": "09/2024", "role": "Developer", "start_time": "09/2024", "titile": "5D Chess"}, "summary": {"_.md": "# 5D Chess (September 2024)\\n\\n**Role:** Developer\\n\\nA personal recreation of *5D Chess with Multiverse Time Travel*, the notoriously complex variant of chess that layers timeline branching and time travel on top of standard chess rules. The project was built in **Unity 3D** using **C#**, completed over the course of September 2024.\\n\\nThe most notable design decision was departing from the original game\'s 2D board-of-boards presentation in favor of a full **3D visualization** of the multiverse of chessboards. In 5D Chess, players don\'t just move pieces across a single board \\u2014 they can move pieces across time, spawning new timelines each time a player \\"jumps\\" to a different point in the game\'s history. The original game represents this as a 2D grid of chessboard \\"tiles,\\" where each tile is one board at one point in time on one timeline, arranged so that time flows along one axis and divergent timelines fan out along another. Recreating this in 3D suggests an attempt to make the spatial relationships between timelines and turns more intuitive or immersive \\u2014 likely arranging boards in three-dimensional space so that time travel and timeline branching can be visualized as physical depth and divergence rather than as a flat grid.\\n\\nBuilding a game like this involves significant technical challenges beyond typical chess engine work:\\n\\n- **Multiverse state management**: tracking many simultaneous board states across branching timelines, since every time-travel move can spawn a new timeline that must be independently simulated and rendered.\\n- **Turn and move legality across timelines**: 5D Chess\'s rules require validating moves not just on a single board, but with respect to check conditions that can span multiple timelines and time periods simultaneously \\u2014 a substantially harder problem than standard chess move validation.\\n- **3D camera and interaction design**: since the game reimagines the board layout in 3D space, the project would have needed custom camera controls, piece selection/movement interactions, and visual conventions for representing time and timeline axes clearly to the player.\\n- **Unity/C# implementation**: leveraging Unity\'s 3D rendering and scene graph to lay out and animate the multiverse of boards, along with custom game logic for chess rules extended to the time-travel variant.\\n\\nGiven the one-month timeframe, the project was likely a scoped hobby/portfolio effort rather than a full-featured clone \\u2014 plausibly focused on demonstrating the core 3D multiverse visualization concept and basic move mechanics rather than exhaustively implementing every rule and edge case of the full 5D Chess ruleset (e.g., all timeline-check interactions, present/past distinctions, or full multiplayer support). No further detail is available on the scope of rules implemented, whether the project is complete or ongoing, or whether it includes multiplayer, AI opponents, or online play.\\n"}}]\n\nInclude everything relevant from both the profile and the full list of projects. Return the complete Markdown document as your result — do not write any files yourself.\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as plain raw text — write the text directly, with no surrounding quotes and no JSON escaping of any kind.'
