2026-09-12 13:09:19,476 INFO fatass: free(): cwd=C:\Users\Qi\Documents\GitHub\fatass\home\courses\prototype\lectures\.next\.next\.next\topics add_dirs=[] silent=True permission_mode=bypassPermissions model=sonnet effort=None tools=Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep prompt='Below is a lecture\'s summary.md content:\n\n# Lecture Summary: Search Procedures — Modeling & Recap\n\n*(Introduction to Methods of Artificial Intelligence, SoSe 2026)*\n\n## Motivation for Search Procedures\n\n- Search appears throughout AI:\n  - **Games**: finding the best move (chess, Go, poker)\n  - **Logic & proofs**: deriving a statement\n  - **Agents**: choosing the optimal next action\n  - **Planning**: finding a sequence of actions to reach a goal\n  - **Optimization**: finding the maximum of a function\n  - **LLMs**: choosing the best next token / best answer\n- **Common structure**: starting from an initial situation, reach a state that satisfies a goal criterion — without knowing the complete search space in advance.\n\n## Representing a Search Problem\n\nThree ingredients define a search problem:\n1. **Initial state** (start state)\n2. **Successor function** N(·) — defines the possible steps/transitions\n3. **Goal test** — decides whether a state is a goal\n\nImportant: the complete search graph is usually **not given explicitly** → we perform **online search** in an **implicitly given graph**.\n\n### Three Classic Examples\n| Problem | Initial state | Successor function | Goal test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Chess | A position | Possible moves | Checkmate |\n| Deduction system | Statement A + axioms | Deduction rules | Proof of A |\n| Route planning | Start location + map/schedule | Connections | Arrived at destination |\n\n## Modern Example: LLM Decoding as a Search Problem\n\nA language model generates text token by token; each step selects from thousands of possible next tokens.\n\n- **Initial state**: the prompt (input tokens)\n- **Successor function**: all tokens with non-negligible probability\n- **Goal test**: `<EOS>` token or maximum length reached\n- **Path cost**: e.g., negative log-probability of the sequence\n\nExample: prompt "The capital of" → branches like "France (0.55) → is (0.7)" vs. "Germany (0.30) → is (0.8) → Bavaria (0.10)".\n\n**Search strategy matters:**\n- **Greedy** decoding: always pick the most probable token (≈ DFS-like, fast, risky)\n- **Beam search**: keep the *k* best prefixes (≈ BFS-like, more expensive, often better)\n\n## Running Example: n-Queens Problem\n\nPlace *n* queens on an *n×n* chessboard so that no queen threatens another.\n\n### Modeling Matters! Three State-Space Representations\n\n| Representation | State definition | State count (n=8) |\n|---|---|---|\n| (A) Naive: all board placements | Any subset of squares occupied by queens | 2^(n²) → 2^64 ≈ 1.8×10^19 |\n| (B) One queen per column | List (r₁,...,rₙ), rᵢ ∈ {1,...,n} | n^n → 8^8 ≈ 1.7×10^7 |\n| (C) Permutations (distinct row per column) | Permutation of (1,...,n) | n! → 8! = 40,320 |\n\n**Take-away**: the choice of representation determines feasibility — representation (C) is about 10^14 times smaller than the naive representation (A).\n\n## Search Space, Search Graph & Properties\n\n**Search graph:**\n- Nodes = states/situations\n- Edges = implicit, defined via successor function N\n- Has a start node and a goal test\n\n**Key metrics:**\n- **Branching factor** b = |N(K)| — number of direct successors\n- **Depth** d of the shallowest goal node\n- **Search space size at depth d** ≈ b^d — the combinatorial explosion\n\n**When is a search strategy good?**\n- **Complete**: finds a goal if one exists\n- **Optimal**: finds the best (e.g., shortest) goal\n- **Resources**: time and space (typically expressed in terms of b and d)\n\nExample: chess has b ≈ 35, depth ≈ 50 → 35^50, more nodes than atoms in the universe.\n\n## Uninformed Search — The Two Classics\n\n"Uninformed" = the strategy uses no information about the goal, only the graph structure.\n\n### Depth-First Search (DFS)\n- Uses a **stack**: most recently generated node first\n- Goes as deep as possible before backtracking\n\n### Breadth-First Search (BFS)\n- Uses a **queue**: oldest node first\n- Explores all nodes at the same depth layer by layer\n\n### Properties: DFS vs. BFS\n\n| | DFS | BFS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Time | O(b^d) | O(b^d) |\n| Space | O(b·d) (linear!) | O(b^d) (exponential!) |\n| Complete? | No (infinite paths possible) | Yes |\n| Optimal? | No | Yes (with equal edge costs) |\n\n**Trade-off in one sentence**: DFS saves memory but can descend infinitely deep; BFS guarantees the shortest path but needs exponential memory.\n\n## Where Uninformed Search Hits Its Limits\n\n- **Chess**: b ≈ 35, useful depth d ≈ 10 → 35^10 ≈ 2.8×10^15 nodes (BFS infeasible)\n- **Route planning in Germany**: ~10 million road nodes — BFS finds the shortest route but wastes time expanding equally in all directions\n- **LLM decoding**: vocabulary ~50,000, sequence lengths 100–1000 → complete/exhaustive search is completely out of the question\n\n**Key observation**: DFS and BFS are "blind" — they have no notion of whether they are getting closer to or farther from the goal. (This motivates the need for *informed* search strategies, to be covered next.)\n\n\nIdentify this lecture\'s major topics (roughly 2-5, whatever is natural for this lecture\'s content — don\'t force a fixed count). For each topic, write a concise overview (a few sentences to a short paragraph, in English) describing that topic.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON list of strings, one overview per topic, in the order the topics appear in the lecture.\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array.'
