2026-09-13 18:12:39,536 INFO fatass: free(): cwd=C:\Users\Qi\Documents\GitHub\fatass\home\courses\prototype\lectures add_dirs=[] silent=True permission_mode=bypassPermissions model=sonnet effort=None tools=Read,Write prompt='Below are numbered overview texts for individual learning \'points\' from a course\'s lecture notes. For EACH numbered overview, write a short, specific title (roughly 3-8 words) that names what that point is actually about — a proper name/label, NOT a restatement or truncation of the opening sentence. For example, for an overview beginning \'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule: the main lecture meets every Thursday...\', a good title is \'Weekly Lecture and Exercise Schedule\', not \'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule\'.\n\n0. The course follows a fixed weekly schedule: the main lecture meets every Thursday from 10:00 to 12:00. In addition, biweekly exercise sessions are held on Fridays, meaning students attend an exercise session every other week rather than weekly. Knowing this cadence is important for planning study time and recognizing that not every Friday will have a session — only alternating ones.\n\n1. Because the exercise sessions are biweekly, the cohort is split into four groups, likely to keep group sizes manageable and to distribute students across the alternating Friday slots. The first exercise session takes place on May 8. Students should identify which of the four groups they belong to and confirm their corresponding session dates in advance.\n\n2. To participate in the exercise sessions, students must register via Moodle, and this registration has a hard deadline of April 30 — before the first session on May 8. Missing this registration deadline could prevent a student from being assigned to a group or session slot, so it is a concrete, actionable checkpoint early in the course.\n\n3. Exercises may be submitted collaboratively in groups of up to three students, submitted through Moodle. This is a specific administrative rule: the maximum group size is three (not more), and the submission channel is exclusively Moodle rather than email or another platform.\n\n4. All course materials, assignments, and registration processes are centralized on the course\'s Moodle page, hosted on studiumdigitale under course ID 9060. Students should know this specific platform and course ID as the single source of truth for logistics, rather than expecting materials to be distributed elsewhere.\n\n5. The stated goal of AI is to build an intelligent agent - ideally one that is autonomous, learning, intelligent, and automatic. Russell & Norvig\'s influential 2x2 classification splits AI approaches along two axes: thinking vs. acting, and humanly vs. rationally. This yields four schools: think humanly (cognitive science, model how humans actually reason), think rationally (the logicist approach using formal logic and deduction), act humanly (behavioral equivalence to a human, epitomized by the Turing Test), and act rationally (build agents that maximize expected outcome). The lecture argues the rational-agent approach is the most general, since it does not require human-likeness as a precondition and can still incorporate the other approaches when they happen to be rational. This is also the approach the course itself follows: an engineering-oriented focus on directly programmable, especially machine-learning-based, AI methods.\n\n6. AI research splits into two broad technical paradigms. The symbolic / physical-symbol-system paradigm relies on explicit programming, logics, rules, and inference procedures - strong for drawing verifiable conclusions, interpretability, and giving mathematical guarantees, but brittle and labor-intensive to hand-engineer. The connectionist paradigm relies on artificial neural networks that learn distributed representations directly from data - strong for perception, pattern recognition, and handling noisy or high-dimensional data, but harder to interpret (\'black box\'). The lecture stresses that complex modern AI systems (AlphaFold, Aristotle, LLM agents) typically combine both paradigms rather than choosing one exclusively. In large language models specifically, the classical separation between \'knowledge representation\' and \'inference mechanism\' (the Smith knowledge-representation hypothesis) breaks down, since both are fused into the same trained network.\n\n7. The lecture highlights several modern AI systems as case studies of combined-paradigm approaches: AlphaGo (2016) beat world champion Lee Sedol at Go using Monte Carlo Tree Search plus neural network position evaluation; AlphaFold solved the decades-old protein-folding problem by combining search with deep learning, earning the Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2024; and Aristotle (Harmonic, 2025) combined Monte Carlo Graph Search, reinforcement learning, LLM-based informal reasoning, and the Lean formal-proof checker to win an IMO-2025 gold-medal-equivalent result (5 of 6 problems, all machine-verified). The lecture also surveys the standard benchmark landscape used to measure progress: MMLU, GPQA Diamond, ARC-AGI-2, Humanity\'s Last Exam, SWE-bench, Chatbot Arena, and AgentBench/tau-bench - while cautioning with Goodhart\'s Law that \'when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\': models can be over-optimized for benchmark scores without a corresponding real capability gain.\n\n8. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting, introduced by Wei et al. (2022), instructs a large language model to lay out its reasoning process step by step before producing a final answer, rather than jumping directly from question to answer. Standard prompting treats the model as a black box (direct question, direct answer, no visible reasoning), whereas CoT prompting makes the intermediate reasoning steps explicit and traceable. The lecture\'s canonical example: asking how much Peter spends buying apples and oranges - standard prompting just outputs the total, while CoT prompting shows the multiplication and addition steps explicitly, which tends to produce more accurate results on complex, multi-step problems.\n\n9. There are two main ways to induce CoT reasoning. Zero-shot CoT simply appends a trigger phrase such as \'Let\'s think step by step\' to the prompt, with no worked examples - this is surprisingly effective, but only once a model exceeds a certain scale (an emergent capability that doesn\'t appear in smaller models). Few-shot CoT instead demonstrates 2-8 example question/reasoning/answer triples before the actual question, so the model imitates the pattern of step-by-step reasoning; this tends to be especially effective on complex reasoning tasks such as arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning.\n\n10. The lecture raises the question of whether CoT reveals genuine \'thinking\' or merely a simulated reasoning trace. Human thinking involves consciousness, intentionality, and grounded experience of meaning; an LLM instead predicts the next token based on statistical patterns learned from training data, with no consciousness or genuine world-knowledge. The lecture frames this as \'simulated\' reproduction of reasoning patterns from training data, versus something \'novel\': functional equivalence, abstraction, and transfer. Practically, regardless of the metaphysical answer, CoT provides real benefits: better accuracy on logical tasks, fewer hallucinations, and more explainable/traceable decisions (relevant to Explainable AI).\n\n11. Materialism holds that nothing exists except matter - mind, thought, and will all arise from matter. Its consequence for AI: since everything that makes up a human, including intelligence and thought, can in principle be studied and analyzed by natural science, everything is in principle constructible, including a thinking, intelligent human-equivalent system. This underwrites the claim that AI is possible in principle.\n\n12. Behaviorism holds that only observable behavior is a valid subject of science; unverifiable or unfalsifiable questions (like \'does it really believe/want/fear something\') are meaningless, since beliefs, ideas, and consciousness are just descriptions of behavior patterns. Consequence: two systems (e.g., a human and a machine) are equivalent if they exhibit the same input/output behavior - real internal understanding is irrelevant to the behaviorist. This grounds the \'weak AI hypothesis\': a system can act as if intelligent without needing to actually be conscious.\n\n13. Functionalism holds that what matters is not what a system is made of but what it does - mental states are defined by their functional/causal role (relations between inputs, other mental states, and behavioral outputs), not by their physical substrate. The lecture\'s example: DAB (digital) and FM (analog) radios are technically completely different but functionally equivalent (both turn a signal into music); by contrast, streaming and radio are not functionally equivalent. Formally, system A in S1 is functionally equivalent to B in S2 if identical inputs produce identical outputs and functionally equivalent successor states - implying that a brain (neurons) and a computer (transistors) could in principle be functionally equivalent. Materialism plus functionalism together support the \'strong AI hypothesis\': that a functionally equivalent machine would really think and be conscious, not just simulate thinking, as opposed to the weak AI hypothesis (behaviorism-supported) that machines merely act as if intelligent.\n\n14. Proposed by Alan Turing as a test for the strong AI hypothesis: a human questioner communicates via text with a hidden human and a hidden computer, without knowing which is which, and tries to identify the computer. If the questioner cannot reliably distinguish computer from human, the test is passed. Criticisms include that the test\'s outcome depends heavily on the questioner\'s own skill and knowledge (not objective), and that it can be gamed by systems built as giant lookup tables of canned responses without any real intelligence (as ELIZA demonstrated). The \'Total Turing Test\' extends the classic version with video transmission and object recognition to test embodied perception as well as language.\n\n15. Searle\'s 1980 Chinese Room argument: a person who understands no Chinese sits in a room, receives Chinese characters through a slot, and uses a rulebook (in their own language) to produce grammatically correct Chinese responses, fooling outside observers into thinking a Chinese speaker is inside. Searle\'s conclusion: the person is manipulating pure syntax without understanding any semantics or meaning, so no part of the system truly \'understands\' Chinese - this is presented as a direct argument against the strong AI hypothesis, i.e. symbol manipulation alone cannot produce genuine understanding. The behaviorist counter-argument: the whole system (person + rulebook + room) does understand, since understanding is observable in its correct outputs, even if no individual component does.\n\n16. The lecture also discusses a set of alternative tests for evaluating whether an AI system is approaching AGI: the coffee-preparation test (a robot must make coffee in an arbitrary kitchen), the IKEA test (assemble a disassembled, packaged IKEA piece of furniture), and the student test (pass standardized human exams such as bar exams or the SAT). The ARC Prize / ARC-AGI benchmark is presented as a rigorous modern AGI benchmark: a $1M+ open-source competition targeting the ARC-AGI benchmark, which measures generalization ability on genuinely novel tasks (not memorizable from training data) via abstraction and reasoning. ARC-AGI-1 targeted basic fluid intelligence, ARC-AGI-2 emphasizes adaptability and efficiency; about 98.7% of ARC tasks are human-solvable, underscoring a persistent gap between human and artificial general intelligence, though note that even ARC-AGI has shown susceptibility to Goodhart\'s-Law-style benchmark gaming as scores rose quickly.\n\n17. An agent is anything that perceives its environment through sensors and acts upon it through actuators. Formally, the agent function maps the sequence of percepts (observations) received so far to an action; this function can be implemented by an agent program. The lecture generalizes this with additional inputs beyond raw observation: prior knowledge (e.g., a map), learned/experiential knowledge (e.g., test cases), and goals/priorities, all feeding into the choice of the next action via reasoning or learning.\n\n18. A rational (intelligent) agent is defined as one that always chooses the optimal action with respect to a performance measure, given its percept sequence and prior knowledge of the environment. Choosing the right performance measure is itself a nontrivial design task, illustrated with Russell & Norvig\'s vacuum-world example: a two-square world where the agent can move, suck up dirt, or do nothing, and different performance measures (e.g., \'always maximally clean\' vs. \'clean but minimize power use\' vs. \'clean but minimize disturbance\') lead to different notions of what counts as the optimal, rational agent.\n\n19. Task environments are classified along several dimensions that determine how hard the agent\'s job is: fully observable vs. partially observable (does the sensor data give complete state information, e.g. a vacuum agent that can only see its own square); deterministic vs. stochastic (does the next state follow with certainty from the current state and action, e.g. dirt appearing randomly); episodic vs. sequential (are episodes independent atomic events, or do current actions affect the future, e.g. chess); static vs. dynamic or semi-dynamic (can the environment change while the agent is still deliberating, e.g. chess with a clock); discrete vs. continuous; and single-agent vs. multi-agent (further split into competitive/adversarial vs. cooperative). The lecture works through Russell & Norvig\'s classic table of example environments (crossword puzzles, chess with a clock, poker, backgammon, taxi driving, medical diagnosis, image analysis, an interactive English tutor) classified along these axes.\n\n20. Classical agent architectures form a hierarchy of increasing sophistication and decreasing reliance on explicit hand-programming: the simple reflex agent maps observation directly to action via a lookup table or condition-action rules with no memory; the model-based reflex agent maintains internal state to track aspects of the world it cannot currently observe; the goal-based agent chooses actions that move it toward an explicit goal and can recognize goal achievement; the utility-based agent uses a quantitative utility function to rank actions, allowing it to handle trade-offs between competing objectives; and the learning agent adds a learning element (plus critic and exploration element) that lets the agent improve its behavior from experience, either online during operation or offline during a training phase. The lecture connects this to LLM-based Foundation Agents, where the actual \'learning\' mostly happens during pretraining, and runtime behavior is instead adapted via prompts, tools, and refinement loops.\n\n21. Foundation models, per the Stanford CRFM (2021) definition, are large models pretrained (usually self-supervised, often multimodally across text, code, image, audio, video) on broad data that serve as a base for many downstream applications via transfer at scale, with parameter counts reaching up to roughly 10^12. Scale itself produces emergent abilities - capabilities that appear abruptly once a critical model size is crossed and were neither specifically trained for nor easy to predict in advance, such as chain-of-thought reasoning, few-shot learning, and instruction-following (e.g., reliable multi-digit addition emerging only around ~13B parameters).\n\n22. Unlike classical agents that map observation directly to action, a Foundation Agent wraps an LLM core with modular extensions: memory/context, tool-use, planning, and reasoning. A key architectural difference from classical agents is the absence of a strict separation between knowledge representation and inference mechanism. Tool-Using is the capability that lets these agents call external systems - search engines and RAG for information retrieval, Python interpreters or simulators for computation, databases/cloud services/browsers for system interaction, and specialized models for image/audio analysis or translation - extending the model beyond what it can do from its weights alone; a key rule of thumb from the lecture is that a tool-using agent is only as capable as the tools it has access to. The ReAct paradigm (\'Reasoning + Acting\') operationalizes this by interleaving explicit reasoning steps with tool-call actions and observations in a loop (e.g., reason about what info is needed, call a search tool, observe result, reason further), which the lecture illustrates with a worked example comparing Grand Slam titles of two tennis players.\n\n23. Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) consist of multiple interacting autonomous agents that jointly solve complex problems by cooperating, competing, or coordinating. Classical (pre-Foundation-Model) MAS relied on limited, structured agent communication and explicit coordination protocols with high development effort, whereas modern LLM-based MAS use flexible natural-language communication and can show emergent coordination with faster development. Benefits include division of labor, specialization, redundancy/robustness, scalability, and emergent collective intelligence exceeding the sum of individual agents. A key technique is Multi-Agent Debate, where agents (e.g., a Proposer and a Critic) argue from different perspectives, critique each other, and converge on a refined consensus - illustrated by the lecture\'s case study, ModelAuditor, a real research system where a Proposer/Critic debate autonomously selects evaluation metrics and simulates distribution shifts to audit clinical AI models cheaply (under $0.50 and 10 minutes per audit) and more thoroughly than individual human practitioners.\n\n24. As agents become more autonomous, aligning them with human intent becomes critical. AI Alignment is the effort to ensure AI systems match human values/intentions, pursue intended goals without unintended harm, and remain safe and controllable as capability grows; core challenges include the specification problem (precisely formalizing human values is hard) and value misalignment (divergence between what the AI optimizes for and what humans actually want). RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) trains a reward model from human rankings of model outputs, then fine-tunes the LLM via RL to maximize that learned reward - used in GPT-4, Claude, and other major LLMs. Constitutional AI instead has the model critique and revise its own outputs against a written set of principles (a \'constitution\'), reducing dependence on large-scale human labeling - used by Anthropic for Claude. Red-Teaming systematically and adversarially probes a system (via human experts, automated AI-vs-AI attacks, or crowdsourcing) to proactively surface harmful, unsafe, or problematic behavior before deployment, complementing RLHF and Constitutional AI as part of a layered alignment strategy. The lecture closes with an applied ethical dilemma (an autonomous traffic-routing assistant deciding whether to reroute traffic through a residential area near a school) to compare how an RLHF-tuned agent, a Constitutional-AI agent, and a multi-agent negotiation system might each resolve the trade-off differently.\n\n25. Search as a common framework: a wide range of AI problems can be cast as search problems, where you begin in some initial state and apply a search procedure to find a state (or sequence of states) satisfying a goal test. The key elements are a state space (the set of all possible configurations), an initial state, one or more actions or operators that move between states, and a goal test that decides when a satisfactory state has been reached. Framing a problem this way lets the same algorithms and analysis tools (e.g., breadth-first, depth-first, best-first search) be reused across domains that otherwise look nothing alike.\n\n26. Search in games: in adversarial settings like chess, Go, or poker, search means exploring possible sequences of moves (a game tree) to find the move that leads to the best outcome, accounting for an opponent who is also choosing moves to their own advantage. This introduces the idea of adversarial search (e.g., minimax, alpha-beta pruning) and, in games with hidden information like poker, the need to reason about probability distributions over hidden state (opponent\'s cards) rather than a single known board position.\n\n27. Search in logic and theorem proving: proving a statement from a set of axioms and inference rules can be viewed as searching through the space of statements derivable from the axioms, applying inference rules as \'moves\' until the target statement (the goal) is reached. Here the search space is often huge or only implicitly defined — rather than being handed all possible derivations, the search procedure generates new candidate statements on the fly, which mirrors the general theme that the full search space is rarely known in advance.\n\n28. Search in agents and planning: an agent choosing its next action can be understood as searching over possible actions for the one that maximizes some notion of value or reward, while planning extends this to searching over sequences of actions that transform an initial situation into one satisfying a goal condition. Planning problems highlight the added complexity of chaining decisions together, since the value of one action depends on which actions follow it, requiring search over multi-step trajectories rather than single choices.\n\n29. Search in optimization: finding the maximum (or minimum) of a function is itself a search problem — the \'state space\' is the function\'s domain, and the search procedure (e.g., hill climbing, gradient-based methods, simulated annealing) navigates that domain looking for a state (input) that best satisfies the goal criterion (highest function value). This reframes numerical optimization as structurally identical to the other search problems, differing mainly in whether the space is discrete or continuous and whether gradient information is available.\n\n30. Search in LLMs: generating text with a large language model can be viewed as searching over the space of possible next tokens (or full answers) for ones that best satisfy an implicit goal, such as high likelihood or alignment with a prompt\'s intent. Techniques like greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling are all search strategies over this token space, and more elaborate approaches (e.g., chain-of-thought or tree-of-thought prompting) explicitly search over multiple candidate reasoning paths before settling on an answer — showing that even modern neural methods inherit the same initial-state-to-goal-state search structure seen elsewhere in AI.\n\n31. A search problem is formally defined by three ingredients: an initial state (the starting point of the search), a successor function N(·) that, given a state, returns the set of possible next states (often along with the action and cost associated with each transition), and a goal test that determines whether a given state counts as a solution. This triple is enough to specify any search problem abstractly, independent of the domain it comes from, which is what makes the framework so widely reusable across chess, theorem proving, route planning, and more.\n\n32. Unlike a graph algorithm that operates on an explicit adjacency list, most real search problems have graphs that are far too large (or infinite) to write down in advance. Instead, the graph is defined implicitly: nodes and edges are generated on demand by calling the successor function only on states actually visited during the search. This \'online\' expansion is what allows search to scale to enormous or unbounded state spaces, such as all possible chess positions or all possible token sequences a language model could generate.\n\n33. The abstract search framework applies to very different-looking problems once you identify the right initial state, successor function, and goal test. In chess, a state is a board position, successors are legal moves, and the goal test checks for checkmate. In deduction systems, a state is a set of known facts or a partial proof, successors apply inference rules, and the goal test checks whether the target statement has been derived. In route planning, a state is a location, successors are reachable neighboring locations (with edge costs like distance or time), and the goal is reaching the destination. Recognizing this shared structure lets the same search algorithms be reused across domains.\n\n34. LLM decoding can itself be cast as a search problem: the initial state is the prompt (the sequence of tokens generated so far), the successor function proposes candidate next tokens weighted by the model\'s predicted probability distribution, and the goal test is satisfied when an end-of-sequence token is produced or a maximum length is reached. The path cost of a generated sequence is defined as the negative log-probability of the tokens along that path, so lower cost corresponds to higher-probability (more likely) sequences. This reframing lets classical search algorithms and their trade-offs be applied directly to the problem of generating text.\n\n35. Greedy decoding behaves like depth-first search: at each step it commits to the single next token with the highest probability and never reconsiders that choice, following one path deeply without backtracking. This makes it fast and cheap since only one hypothesis is tracked at a time, but it is risky because an early locally-optimal token choice can lock the search into a globally suboptimal or low-quality sequence, with no way to recover.\n\n36. Beam search behaves more like breadth-first search: instead of keeping only one hypothesis, it maintains a fixed number k (the beam width) of the most promising partial sequences at each step, expanding all of them and pruning down to the top k by cumulative path cost (negative log-probability) after each expansion. This explores more of the search space than greedy decoding and typically produces better-scoring sequences, but at a proportionally higher computational cost since k hypotheses must be expanded and scored at every step instead of just one.\n\n37. The n-Queens problem asks how to place n queens on an n×n chessboard so that no two queens attack each other (no shared row, column, or diagonal). While the goal itself is simple to state, how one chooses to represent a \'state\' of the search — a partial or complete arrangement of queens — is not fixed by the problem statement. The lecture uses n-Queens precisely because it is simple enough to reason about by hand, yet rich enough to expose how dramatically the size of the search space can change depending on modeling choices, making it an ideal vehicle for teaching state-space design.\n\n38. The most naive representation treats a state as an arbitrary subset of the board\'s n^2 squares, where each square is either occupied by a queen or empty, with no built-in constraints on how many queens are placed or where. This yields 2^(n^2) possible states, since each of the n^2 squares independently can be \'queen\' or \'no queen\'. For n=8 this is 2^64, an astronomically large number that makes brute-force search over this state space computationally infeasible, even though the actual solutions we care about occupy a tiny corner of this space.\n\n39. A smarter representation exploits the fact that any valid solution must have exactly one queen per column (since two queens sharing a column always attack each other). By restricting the state space to only those configurations with exactly one queen per column, each of the n columns independently chooses one of n possible rows, giving n^n total states. For n=8 this is 8^8 (about 16.7 million), a massive reduction from 2^64, illustrating how encoding a known constraint directly into the representation — rather than leaving it to be discovered during search — shrinks the space by many orders of magnitude.\n\n40. An even tighter representation additionally exploits the fact that no two queens can share a row either, so a valid solution must assign each column a distinct row — i.e., a permutation of the row indices 1..n. Representing states as permutations (bijective column-to-row mappings) reduces the state space to n! (8! = 40,320 for n=8), factoring out not just the column constraint but also the row constraint. This is dramatically smaller than both the naive (2^64) and one-queen-per-column (8^8) representations, though diagonal-attack constraints still must be checked during search since they are not encoded into the representation itself.\n\n41. The overarching lesson is that the choice of state-space representation is often more decisive for tractability than the choice of search algorithm. By building known problem constraints (one queen per column, one queen per row) directly into what counts as a \'state,\' the search space shrinks from 2^(n^2) to n^n to n!, illustrating a general principle in AI and algorithm design: a well-chosen representation can turn an intractable brute-force problem into a practically solvable one, and modeling decisions should be considered as carefully as the search or optimization procedure applied afterward.\n\n42. A search graph is the abstract structure underlying problem-solving search: nodes represent states or situations, and edges are not stored explicitly but are generated on demand by a successor function that produces the states reachable from a given state (e.g. legal moves from a board position). Every search problem is defined by this implicit graph together with a designated start node (the initial state) and a goal test, a predicate that determines whether a given node counts as a solution. Because the graph is implicit, a search algorithm must generate nodes as it goes rather than reading them from a fixed data structure, and the same underlying state space can be searched in many different orders depending on the strategy used to decide which node to expand next.\n\n43. The branching factor b is the number of direct successors (children) a typical node has, i.e. how many actions or moves are available from a given state. The depth d refers to the depth of the shallowest goal node in the search tree, i.e. the length of the shortest path from the start node to a solution. These two quantities are the standard way of characterizing the size and difficulty of a search problem, and nearly all complexity analysis of search algorithms is expressed as a function of b and d (or b and m, the maximum depth, for algorithms without a known solution depth).\n\n44. Because each node at a given depth spawns roughly b new nodes at the next depth, the number of nodes at depth d grows approximately as b^d — exponential in the depth. This combinatorial explosion means that even modest branching factors and depths can produce astronomically large search spaces: doubling the depth does not double the work, it roughly squares it. Understanding this formula is essential for predicting when a naive search (like brute-force exhaustive search) will become computationally infeasible, and it motivates the need for smarter search strategies, heuristics, and pruning techniques.\n\n45. Chess is used as a concrete illustration of exponential blowup: with an average branching factor of about b≈35 (roughly 35 legal moves available in a typical position) and a search depth of around 50 ply (moves) needed to reach a decisive endgame, the number of nodes in the resulting search tree (about 35^50) vastly exceeds the estimated number of atoms in the observable universe (~10^80). This example makes vivid why exhaustive search is not a viable strategy for non-trivial problems and why real chess engines rely on heuristics, pruning (e.g. alpha-beta pruning), and limited-depth evaluation rather than exploring the full game tree.\n\n46. A good search strategy is ideally evaluated on several criteria: completeness (does it always find a goal node if one exists, even in infinite or very large spaces?), optimality (does it find the best goal, e.g. the shortest or lowest-cost path, rather than just any goal?), time complexity (how many nodes must be generated or expanded, typically expressed as a function of b and d), and space complexity (how much memory is needed to store the frontier and/or explored nodes, also typically a function of b and d). These four criteria are used throughout the study of search algorithms to compare strategies like breadth-first, depth-first, uniform-cost, and informed (heuristic) search, since no single strategy is uniformly best — each makes different trade-offs among completeness, optimality, time, and space.\n\n47. Depth-first search (DFS) explores by always expanding the most recently generated node, implemented using a stack (either explicitly or via recursion). This means it plunges as deep as possible along one branch before backtracking to try alternatives. Its major advantage is memory efficiency: it only needs to store the nodes along the current path plus their unexplored siblings, giving a space complexity of O(b·d), where b is the branching factor and d is the depth of the path. However, DFS is neither complete nor optimal in general graphs: if the search space contains infinite paths or cycles (without cycle checking), DFS can descend forever down one branch and never find a goal even if one exists elsewhere, and even when it does find a goal, that goal is not guaranteed to be reached via the shortest or cheapest path, since DFS commits to depth over breadth.\n\n48. Breadth-first search (BFS) explores the search space level by level, expanding all nodes at a given depth before moving to the next depth, using a queue (first-in, first-out) to manage the frontier of generated-but-unexpanded nodes. Because it exhausts each shallower layer before going deeper, BFS is guaranteed to find a solution if one exists (completeness), and when all edge costs are equal (uniform-cost edges), the first goal it finds is guaranteed to be reachable via the fewest edges, making it optimal under that condition. The price for these guarantees is space: BFS must keep an entire frontier of nodes in memory simultaneously, which grows exponentially with depth as O(b^d), where b is the branching factor and d is the solution depth — this exponential memory requirement is what makes BFS impractical for large or deep search spaces despite its correctness guarantees.\n\n49. DFS and BFS are both classified as \'uninformed\' (or \'blind\') search strategies because they operate using only the raw structure of the search graph — which nodes exist and how they connect — with no additional knowledge about which nodes are more promising or closer to the goal. Neither algorithm can tell, when choosing which node to expand next, whether that choice is moving the search closer to or farther from the goal; they simply follow their fixed rule (last-in stack order for DFS, first-in queue order for BFS) regardless of how far any given node actually is from a solution. This blindness is precisely what distinguishes them from informed (heuristic-based) search strategies, and it is the central limitation that motivates introducing heuristics and informed search methods, which use estimates of distance-to-goal to guide expansion more efficiently.\n\n50. The impracticality of uninformed search on large problems can be made concrete with real branching-factor and depth numbers. In chess, the branching factor is roughly 35 legal moves per position, so exploring even a modest number of plies ahead produces an astronomically large search tree, quickly exceeding any feasible memory or time budget for BFS and making even DFS impractical to run to completion. In real-world route planning over a road network (e.g., Germany), while the branching factor per intersection is small, the depth (number of steps) needed to reach distant destinations, combined with the sheer size of the graph, means naive uninformed search explores far more of the map than necessary, motivating more targeted methods. In LLM decoding, the \'branching factor\' at each generation step is the vocabulary size (on the order of ~50,000 possible tokens), and sequences run 100 to 1000 tokens long — exhaustively searching this space with BFS or DFS is utterly infeasible (b^d is inconceivably large), which is why practical decoding uses greedy, sampling, or beam search rather than exhaustive uninformed search.\n\n51. Taken together, these examples establish the core trade-off between DFS and BFS and set up the motivation for informed search. DFS is cheap in space (O(b·d)) but risks incompleteness and non-optimal solutions; BFS guarantees completeness and optimality (for uniform costs) but is expensive in space (O(b^d)). Both, being uninformed, waste effort exploring directions that domain knowledge could immediately rule out as unpromising. The chess, road-network, and LLM examples show that realistic branching factors and depths push both algorithms\' costs beyond feasibility, which is the practical argument for moving to informed search strategies (such as greedy best-first search or A*) that use a heuristic function to estimate distance to the goal and prioritize expansion accordingly, avoiding the wasted blind exploration inherent to DFS and BFS.\n\n52. A heuristic function h(n) is an estimate of the remaining cost/distance from node n to the goal, distinguishing informed search from uninformed (blind) methods like DFS/BFS which have no sense of direction. In the lecture\'s convention, goal nodes maximize (or, in later A* material, minimize) the relevant evaluation function. Typical real-world analogues given in the slides include straight-line (Euclidean) distance for route planning and expected completion quality in LLM decoding.\n\n53. The 8-puzzle example in the lecture compares two heuristics: f1 counts tiles in the wrong position, and f2 approximates the number of moves needed (a Manhattan-distance-style measure). Even though both estimate \'distance to goal\', f2 is shown to be a more accurate (better informed) predictor for the same states, foreshadowing the later lecture\'s formal notion of heuristics being \'better informed\' than one another.\n\n54. The algorithm maintains a list L of candidate nodes sorted by h; at each step it expands the best current node\'s successors, discards already-visited ones, sorts them by h, and prepends them to the remaining list. This makes it equivalent to a depth-first search that is \'steered\' by the heuristic and shares visited-state information, rather than a true global best-first strategy.\n\n55. Because hill-climbing only accepts moves that strictly improve h, it stops as soon as it reaches a state whose neighbors are all worse — a local maximum — regardless of whether a better (global) maximum exists elsewhere in the search space. This is presented as the algorithm\'s central weakness, illustrated with a \'state space vs. h\' diagram showing the search halting well short of the global maximum.\n\n56. The lecture lists three remedies for getting stuck in local maxima: random restarts (rerun hill-climbing from multiple random starting points), best-first search (retain all previously seen alternatives instead of discarding them), and simulated annealing (allow occasional downhill moves with some probability). These three ideas are each developed further later in the lecture.\n\n57. A minimal Python implementation is given: iteratively pick the best-scoring successor via max(neighbors, key=h); if it is no better than the current node, stop and return the current node (a local maximum), otherwise move to it. This models hill-climbing as a pure function-optimization loop without an explicit stack or goal test.\n\n58. Unlike hill-climbing, which only compares the newest successors against each other, best-first search maintains a single globally sorted list of all nodes seen so far and always expands the overall best one. This is the key structural change that lets it escape local maxima that would trap hill-climbing, since a better alternative elsewhere in the list is never discarded and can be picked up later.\n\n59. The algorithm keeps an Open list sorted best-first; each iteration takes the best node, checks the goal test, computes its successors, merges them into the list (skipping already-visited nodes), and re-sorts. A Python sketch using heapq shows this as a priority-queue-driven search over (h-value, path) pairs — essentially a greedy best-first search implementation.\n\n60. The lecture\'s comparison table contrasts hill-climbing and best-first on: what gets sorted (only new successors vs. the entire frontier), behavior at local maxima (stuck vs. can escape), completeness and optimality (neither is complete/optimal in general), memory (both exponential in depth), and speed (hill-climbing is faster since best-first pays a sorting cost). It also draws the analogy: hill-climbing ≈ greedy decoding, best-first ≈ beam search (width k) in LLMs.\n\n61. An empirical comparison on ~9500 random 8-puzzle start states shows that a more informative heuristic (Manhattan distance, h2) dramatically outperforms a weaker one (misplaced tiles, h1) — mean/median/max runtimes drop by roughly 20x to over 1000x — with no change to the search algorithm itself. The lecture\'s takeaway is that heuristic design is a first-order design decision, not a minor tuning knob.\n\n62. Modeled on the metallurgical process of heating then slowly cooling metal to reach a low-energy crystal configuration, simulated annealing accepts downhill moves with probability p = e^(−ΔE/T), where ΔE is the loss in quality and T is the current temperature. High T early on allows large, exploratory jumps that escape local maxima; as T decreases via a schedule (e.g., T_k = T_0 · α^k), the search increasingly behaves like plain hill-climbing and converges near a hopefully-global optimum.\n\n63. The lecture lists real-world uses of simulated annealing (circuit-board/PCB layout, the Traveling Salesman Problem, historical neural-network training, hyperparameter search) and draws an explicit parallel between its temperature schedule and practices elsewhere in ML: SGD learning-rate warmup/decay, exploration-vs-exploitation trade-offs in reinforcement learning, and temperature-controlled sampling in LLM decoding/beam search.\n\n64. A* maintains an Open set (frontier) and a Closed set (expanded nodes), tracking the best-known cost g(N) for each node reached. At each step it expands the Open node with minimal f(N)=g(N)+h(N); if it is a goal, the search stops. Otherwise, its successors are evaluated: if a successor is reached more cheaply than any previously recorded path, its g-value is updated and it is (re-)inserted into Open, even pulling it back out of Closed if necessary. A worked routing example in the slides shows a case where a node must be moved back from Closed into Open after a cheaper path is discovered.\n\n65. A* is not automatically correct: if the heuristic overestimates the true remaining cost (is not admissible), A* can return a suboptimal path, as shown by a small counterexample graph in the slides. The full list of preconditions for guaranteed optimality includes: only finitely many nodes N satisfy g*(N)+h(N) ≤ d (the optimal cost); h never overestimates the true remaining cost (admissible); every node has finitely many successors; every edge has strictly positive cost; and the graph is simple (at most one edge between any two nodes). Degenerate examples with infinite chains of decreasing edge costs are used to show why the \'finitely many nodes below the bound\' condition cannot be dropped even when h is admissible.\n\n66. Given the preconditions hold, the lecture states and sketches the proof (via induction/lemmas about the infimum cost, optimal nodes always being in Open, expanded goal nodes being optimal, and a goal eventually being expanded) of the theorem that A* finds an optimal path whenever one exists. It also notes two special cases: h(N)=0 for all N reduces A* to uniform-cost / branch-and-bound search, and if additionally all edges have equal cost, A* becomes plain breadth-first search. A variant, A*o, is introduced that finds *all* optimal paths rather than stopping at the first one found.\n\n67. A heuristic h is monotone (consistent) if h(N) ≤ c(N,N′) + h(N′) for every edge to a successor N′, and h(Z)=0 for goals — essentially a triangle inequality, satisfied for example by straight-line distance. Monotonicity implies admissibility, and crucially guarantees that A* assigns each node its final, optimal g-value the very first time it is expanded, so nodes never need to be reopened from Closed. This yields nodes being expanded in non-decreasing f-order and a complexity bound of O(|D|) (or O(c^(d/δ)) with constant branching factor c, optimal cost d, and minimum edge gap δ) when h is O(1) to compute. It also enables a memory-saving tree-search variant of A* (no Closed list, no g-value updates) that remains optimal only when h is monotone.\n\n68. The lecture closes with A* in autonomous driving: a hierarchical planning stack (strategic, tactical, operational, control levels) uses A* variants at multiple abstraction levels; Waymo is cited as combining modified global A*, lattice-based local A*, precomputed traffic-informed heuristics, and multi-criteria optimization (time, comfort, fuel). Anytime Repairing A* (ARA*) is introduced as a way to always have *some* path available and improve it over time by starting with an overestimating heuristic and shrinking its overestimation factor — important for safety-critical, real-time systems. The lecture also mentions learned/neural heuristics (CNNs, RL, GNNs, transfer learning) as an active research direction combining A* with deep learning.\n\n69. A game tree models a two-player, chance-free game as a tree of states (nodes), where the root is the current situation, each node is labeled with whose turn it is (Maximizer or Minimizer), and children represent states reachable by one legal move with the turn alternating. Leaves are terminal states labeled with a game outcome value (e.g. +1 if Maximizer wins, -1 if Minimizer wins, 0 for a draw in chess/TicTacToe). This structure is the foundation for all subsequent search algorithms (MinMax, Alpha-Beta, expectiminimax, MCTS) discussed in the lecture.\n\n70. The MinMax algorithm computes the optimal move by recursion: `Minmax(state, player)` returns the leaf\'s value if there are no successor states; otherwise it recursively evaluates all successor states and returns the maximum of these values if it is the Maximizer\'s turn, or the minimum if it is the Minimizer\'s turn. Values are propagated bottom-up from the leaves to the root, level by level, and the chosen moves are remembered along the way so the actual optimal next move can be reported, not just its value.\n\n71. Plain MinMax explores the full game tree depth-first, with runtime O(c^d) for branching factor c and depth d — for real games like chess this is computationally infeasible even though TicTacToe (9!≈363k sequences, 3^9≈19.7k distinct positions) is just barely tractable. The fix is depth-limited MinMax: search only to a bounded depth k, and evaluate the resulting non-terminal nodes with a heuristic evaluation function (e.g. material/positional advantage in chess, material/mill-count/mobility in Nine Men\'s Morris) instead of a true game-theoretic value. This trades exactness for tractability, with runtime O(c^m) for depth bound m assuming constant-time heuristics.\n\n72. In a chance-free game, only the relative ordering of the evaluation function\'s values across positions determines the optimal move — not the exact numeric values. For example, evaluations Win=1/Draw=0/Loss=-1 and Win=100/Draw=50/Loss=10 both rank Win > Draw > Loss and thus yield the same optimal move, whereas Win=1/Draw=0/Loss=0 (Win > Draw = Loss) has a different ordering and can lead to a different optimal move. This property justifies treating evaluation function design as primarily a matter of getting the ranking of situations right, and it later contrasts with games involving chance (expectiminimax), where exact values do matter.\n\n73. The Alpha-Beta algorithm augments MinMax with two bounds: α (the best value the Maximizer can currently guarantee) and β (the best value the Minimizer can currently guarantee), initially called as AlphaBeta(state, player, -∞, +∞). While iterating over a Minimizer node\'s successors, it tracks the running minimum βl and stops early (cutoff) once βl ≤ α, since the Maximizer would never let play reach this branch; the symmetric rule applies for Maximizer nodes using α and β. This yields exactly the same move choice as plain MinMax, just faster.\n\n74. Alpha-Beta\'s worst-case runtime is the same as plain MinMax, O(c^d), but with good move ordering — examining the strongest moves first — it drops to about O(c^(d/2)), roughly halving the effective branching factor and letting the search go twice as deep for the same computational resources. With purely random move ordering, the runtime is closer to O(c^(3d/4)). Because move order strongly affects pruning effectiveness, a common practical remedy is to pre-sort successor moves using a fast heuristic before recursing.\n\n75. The horizon effect occurs when a fixed search depth cuts off exploration right before a critical good or bad move would be revealed, causing the algorithm to overlook it. A mitigation strategy is to search selectively deeper: if a move at node K is clearly much better than its siblings (or the position involves zugzwang, i.e. being forced to move is disadvantageous), extend the search along that branch further, effectively widening the search horizon there without expanding the overall search space uniformly. Other standard optimizations include caching evaluated positions (transposition tables) and exploiting board symmetries.\n\n76. In games with random elements, the naive approach of running plain MinMax/Alpha-Beta over every possible chance outcome is possible but wasteful. The better approach, known as expectiminimax, adds chance nodes to the game tree between Max and Min layers: instead of taking a max or min over children, a chance node computes the expected value — a probability-weighted average of the children\'s values (e.g. summing p_i × value_i over each possible dice outcome i). This lets the search properly account for the probabilistic structure of games like backgammon.\n\n77. Unlike chance-free games, when probability is involved the move with the best expected value depends on the exact numeric values of the evaluation function, not merely their relative ordering. A worked counterexample in the lecture shows that changing the evaluation values assigned to two leaves (while keeping their relative order the same) changes which parent choice yields the higher expected value, hence changes the optimal move. Practical consequence: evaluation functions for stochastic games must be chosen carefully so they approximate the true expected value of winning as closely as possible, not just get comparisons right.\n\n78. MCTS departs from classical search in three ways: it samples the search space via random simulations instead of exhaustively exploring it, it uses simulation (playouts) rather than a static evaluation function to judge non-terminal states, and it expands the tree asymmetrically, investing more effort in promising branches rather than treating all branches equally. This makes it especially effective for games with a very high branching factor, such as Go (~250 legal moves per position) versus chess (~35), where classical Alpha-Beta search becomes impractical.\n\n79. Each MCTS iteration runs four phases: (1) Selection — traverse the tree from the root, choosing child nodes according to a selection policy, until reaching an expandable leaf; (2) Expansion — add one or more new child nodes to the tree at that leaf; (3) Simulation — run a random playout (rollout) from the new node to a terminal game state; (4) Backpropagation — propagate the simulation\'s result back up the path to the root, updating each visited node\'s win/visit statistics. Repeating this loop many times builds up statistics that let the algorithm converge on strong moves without exhaustive search.\n\n80. MCTS\'s selection phase typically uses UCT (Upper Confidence bound for Trees): UCT(v) = w_v/n_v + c·√(ln N_v / n_v), where w_v is the number of wins through node v, n_v is the number of simulations through v, N_v is the number of simulations through v\'s parent, and c is a tuning constant. The first term rewards exploitation of nodes with a good win rate so far; the second term rewards exploration of less-visited nodes (its value shrinks as n_v grows, and grows as sibling visits N_v grow). Balancing these two terms is what allows MCTS to focus search on promising moves while still occasionally investigating under-explored ones.\n\n81. AlphaGo (DeepMind) defeated Lee Sedol 4:1 in 2016 and AlphaGo Master defeated world #1 Ke Jie in 2017 — the first computer program to beat a top human Go player without a handicap. Go was long considered especially hard for AI due to its huge state space (~10^170 positions vs. ~10^50 for chess), high branching factor (~250 vs. ~35 for chess), and the difficulty of evaluating positions even for human experts — making classical Alpha-Beta/MinMax impractical. AlphaGo\'s breakthrough was combining MCTS with deep neural networks.\n\n82. AlphaGo combines MCTS with two deep neural networks: a policy network, trained via supervised learning on expert human games (and further refined by reinforcement learning), which proposes promising moves and replaces the purely random move selection of traditional MCTS; and a value network, which estimates the win probability of a position directly, reducing reliance on full random playout simulations for evaluation. MCTS uses both networks together — the policy network guides tree expansion/move selection, and the value network (combined with rollout evaluation) scores leaf nodes — to select the best move.\n\n83. Beyond classical board games, MCTS is applied to decision-making problems (autonomous driving path planning, robotics, multi-agent systems) and optimization problems (combinatorial optimization, Bayesian optimization, hyperparameter search for machine learning models). MCTS is especially valuable when the problem space is too large for complete exploration but simulations/evaluations can be run efficiently. The lecture also draws a conceptual link between MCTS\'s exploration/exploitation balance and sampling strategies used in LLM text decoding, where there is a similar trade-off between diversity and quality of generated tokens.\n\n84. An evolutionary algorithm\'s core data structures are: an individual, usually encoded as a bitstring (chromosome), with sub-sequences called genes; a fitness function scoring how good an individual is; and a population, a (multi-)set of individuals. The algorithm iteratively produces a new population from the current one — analogous to breeding animals for a trait, e.g., repeatedly selecting and pairing pigs with the largest ears, hoping the trait spreads through the population.\n\n85. Biological evolution has no explicit optimization goal. Recombination drives diversification of individuals and spreads genes through the population, which helps resilience to infection or fluctuating environments (hunger, heat, cold). Importantly, optimizing one trait can worsen another — e.g., pigs bred for huge ears may end up limping — a reminder that fitness functions in artificial EAs also involve trade-offs.\n\n86. The general genetic-search algorithm: start from an initial population S; while no individual reaches maximal fitness, build a new population S\' by repeatedly (K times) selecting two fitness-weighted parents A and B from S, recombining them into offspring C, mutating C with low probability into C\', and inserting C\' into S\'; then replace S with S\'. The output is the fittest individual found (in the final generation). Selection uses randomness weighted by fitness so higher-fitness individuals are more likely to reproduce.\n\n87. Selection chooses which individuals from generation n contribute to generation n+1, with higher fitness giving a higher probability of being chosen. Selection probability can be assigned proportionally to raw fitness (sensitive to exact fitness values and very flat near optima/plateaus), proportional to an individual\'s rank within the population (e.g. best is 2–3x more likely than worst, tolerating a less precise fitness function), or proportional to a normalized fitness (e.g., fitness minus the minimum fitness in the population).\n\n88. Recombination (crossover) selects two parents (via selection), picks a random cut point in the bitstring, and splices the two parents together at that point to build an offspring. Mutation randomly flips individual bits of a chromosome (0→1 or 1→0), typically with a low probability, to introduce novel variation the population may not already contain. Extinction removes individuals (the worst, or a random one) when the population grows too large; evolution stops once an optimal individual is likely found.\n\n89. A genetic algorithm operates purely on bitstrings, where any bitstring is a valid result of the operators. An evolutionary algorithm generalizes this: it may use other sequence types (e.g., sequences of integers), restrict individuals to only valid/meaningful sequences, and use operators specifically adapted to only ever produce valid offspring (e.g., operators that only yield permutations). Practical parameters to tune include mutation and crossover probabilities, how offspring count depends on fitness, and population size.\n\n90. Queens can be encoded as a sequence of n numbers in {1,...,n}, where the i-th number gives the row of the queen placed in column i, e.g. [3,2,4,2,7,6,8,5]. This encoding allows invalid configurations, since a repeated value (here, 2 appears twice) means two queens share a column. Fitness is defined as the number of non-attacking queen pairs: worst possible value is 0 (every pair threatens each other), and the best possible value is C(n,2) = n(n-1)/2, achieved by a valid, non-attacking solution.\n\n91. With the free encoding, mutation randomly changes one value in the sequence to another value in {1,...,n} (moving one queen within its column/row), and recombination cuts two parent sequences at a point and splices them, which can also generate invalid (duplicate-containing) offspring. A better encoding restricts individuals to permutations of [1,...,n], guaranteeing exactly one queen per row/column; but then recombination almost always yields an invalid permutation, requiring fixes such as immediate extinction of invalid offspring, random repair, or simply skipping recombination — leaving mutation (swapping two elements) as the main operator, which behaves similarly to hill-climbing/best-first search.\n\n92. Consider a fixed gene G (a bit pattern at a fixed position). Let V be the whole population and S the individuals carrying G, with fitness a for members of S and lower fitness b for members of V\\S. If the next generation is formed by drawing |V| individuals from V with replacement, weighted by fitness, the probability of drawing an individual from S is p(S) = a|S| / (a|S| + b(|V|-|S|)), and from V\\S is the complementary expression. The expected size of the new S is p(S)·|V|, and S grows in relative terms whenever a > b.\n\n93. Defining rel(S(t)) as the relative share of S in generation t gives the recursion rel(S(t+1)) = a·rel(S(t)) / (a·rel(S(t)) + b·(1-rel(S(t)))). For the concrete case a = 2b (the gene is twice as fit) this simplifies to rel(t+1) = 2·rel(t)/(rel(t)+1); starting at rel(S(1)) = 10%, the share climbs 10% → 18% → 31% → 47% → 64% → 78% → 88% → 93% → 97% → 98% → 99%, permanently exceeding 90% by about generation 7. This numerically demonstrates how quickly a fitness advantage propagates through a population under pure selection.\n\n94. In the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP), n cities with connections must be toured via the shortest route visiting every city exactly once. An evolutionary approach encodes an individual as a permutation of all cities (the tour order), with fitness as the negative tour length (to be maximized). Mutation must preserve the permutation property, e.g. by swapping two cities\' positions, and recombination needs a repair mechanism since naive crossover of two permutations usually yields an invalid tour; one proposed operator is partial inversion — inverting a sub-tour.\n\n95. The transportation problem (after Michalewicz, 1992) ships goods from warehouses with fixed stock to destinations with fixed demand at given per-unit costs, minimizing total cost (a worked instance with 3 warehouses and 4 destinations has optimal cost 315). Two encodings are possible: (1) the solution matrix itself as the individual — exact, but many individuals (including most produced by mutation/crossover) are invalid; or (2) a matrix of priorities (a permutation of 1..12) — always valid by construction, but crossover breaks the permutation and needs repair. Operators include moving a unit of goods between routes (encoding 1), or swapping/inverting priorities (encoding 2).\n\n96. NASA\'s Space Technology 5 (ST5) mission used an evolutionary algorithm to design the wire shape of an X-band antenna meeting difficult requirements (wide beamwidth for circular polarization plus wide bandwidth). The individual encoded antenna geometry, and fitness was based on radiation performance. The evolved design was a bizarre, \'bent\' shape no human engineer would have proposed, yet it outperformed a traditionally hand-designed antenna — becoming the first computer-evolved hardware flown in space (launched March 2006).\n\n97. Neural Architecture Search (NAS) uses evolutionary search to design neural network architectures instead of hand-crafting them: the individual is a network architecture (layers and connections), fitness is validation accuracy after brief training, and mutation adds/removes layers or swaps operations. AmoebaNet (Real et al., 2019) is a landmark example — an evolutionarily-discovered architecture that was, at the time, state of the art for image classification, directly connecting to the machine-learning material covered elsewhere in the course.\n\n98. Evolution Strategies (OpenAI, 2017) offer an alternative to gradient-based training of reinforcement learning agents: the individual is a policy\'s parameters (weights), and fitness is the reward achieved in the environment, requiring no backpropagation — just \'try and score.\' Compared to gradient methods, which need a differentiable objective and are often faster per step but sensitive to noisy rewards, evolutionary methods need only an evaluation, are extremely parallelizable, and are robust to noise — leading to the rule of thumb: use gradients when available, evolution when not.\n\n99. FunSearch and AlphaEvolve (Google DeepMind, 2023/2025) combine a large language model with evolutionary search to discover algorithms: the individual is a small program, mutation is the LLM proposing code changes (replacing random bit flips), fitness is an automated test scoring the program, and selection keeps the best programs for further evolution. This process has produced new mathematical results and faster algorithms, with evolution supplying the search process and the LLM supplying \'intelligent\' mutations — turning a 30-year-old technique into a cutting-edge AI-driven discovery tool.\n\n100. Machine learning (ML) is a subfield of artificial intelligence, with deep learning in turn a subfield of ML. Tom Mitchell\'s classic definition frames ML as a computer program that learns from experience E with respect to some class of tasks T and performance measure P, improving its performance at T as measured by P through experience. The key distinguishing idea is that ML systems build predictive models directly from data rather than being explicitly programmed with hand-crafted rules: instead of a human specifying the logic step by step, the algorithm infers patterns and relationships from examples, producing a model that can generalize to new, unseen data.\n\n101. ML approaches are commonly divided into three paradigms. Supervised learning trains a model on labeled examples (input-output pairs) to predict outputs for new inputs, covering tasks like classification and regression. Unsupervised learning works with unlabeled data to discover hidden structure, such as clustering similar items or reducing dimensionality. Reinforcement learning trains an agent to make sequential decisions by interacting with an environment, receiving rewards or penalties, and learning a policy that maximizes cumulative reward over time. Understanding which paradigm applies is essential because it determines what kind of data is needed and what kind of model and evaluation are appropriate.\n\n102. Modern ML has enabled a range of prominent real-world applications that illustrate the different paradigms in action: large language models (trained largely via self-supervised and supervised techniques on massive text corpora) power conversational AI and text generation; AlphaFold uses deep learning to predict protein 3D structures from amino acid sequences, transforming structural biology; medical diagnosis systems classify patient data (images, lab results) to assist clinicians in detecting disease; and recommender systems use patterns in user behavior to predict preferences and suggest relevant items, as seen on streaming and e-commerce platforms.\n\n103. Classification is a supervised learning task where the goal is to predict a discrete class label for an object based on its attributes (features). For example, given measurements of an email (word frequencies, sender information), a classifier predicts whether it is \'spam\' or \'not spam\'. To build and evaluate such a model, data is typically split into a training set (used to fit the model) and a test set (held out to assess how well the model generalizes to new data). Stratification ensures that this split preserves the original proportion of each class in both subsets, which is especially important for imbalanced datasets. Cross-validation extends this idea by repeatedly partitioning the data into multiple train/test folds, training and evaluating the model on each fold, and averaging the results to obtain a more robust estimate of model performance and reduce the risk of a lucky or unlucky single split.\n\n104. When evaluating a binary classifier, predictions are categorized using a confusion matrix into four outcomes: true positives (TP, correctly predicted positive cases), true negatives (TN, correctly predicted negative cases), false positives (FP, negative cases incorrectly predicted as positive, i.e., a \'false alarm\'), and false negatives (FN, positive cases incorrectly predicted as negative, i.e., a \'miss\'). The pregnancy-test example illustrates this clearly: a true positive is a pregnant person correctly testing positive, a false positive is a non-pregnant person incorrectly testing positive, a true negative is a non-pregnant person correctly testing negative, and a false negative is a pregnant person incorrectly testing negative. These four counts form the foundation for computing further metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and specificity.\n\n105. The confusion matrix (true positives, false positives, true negatives, false negatives) underlies a family of evaluation metrics, each answering a different question about classifier performance. Recall (sensitivity) is TP / (TP + FN): of all actual positive cases, what fraction did the classifier catch? Specificity is TN / (TN + FP): of all actual negative cases, what fraction did the classifier correctly reject? On the pregnancy-test example, recall measures how many truly pregnant women test positive, while specificity measures how many truly non-pregnant women test negative. These two metrics focus on performance conditioned on the true label, and a classifier can score very differently on each — a test that always predicts \'positive\' has perfect recall but zero specificity.\n\n106. Precision is TP / (TP + FP): of all cases the classifier predicted positive, what fraction were actually positive? Negative predictive value (NPV) is TN / (TN + FN): of all cases predicted negative, what fraction were actually negative? Unlike recall and specificity, which condition on the true label, precision and NPV condition on the predicted label, capturing how trustworthy a positive or negative prediction actually is. On the pregnancy-test example, precision asks: among women who tested positive, how many are truly pregnant? Precision is especially sensitive to class imbalance and the base rate of the positive class.\n\n107. The F-measure (F1 score) is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall). The harmonic mean is used instead of the arithmetic mean because it penalizes extreme imbalance between the two components — a classifier can only score well on F1 if both precision and recall are reasonably high, not just one of them. This makes F1 a useful single-number summary when both false positives and false negatives matter and no single metric like accuracy alone captures the tradeoff.\n\n108. The rare-disease (\'yellow fever\' fever-based classifier) example illustrates a key pitfall: when the positive class is rare, a classifier that flags almost everyone with a fever as having the disease can achieve very high recall (catching nearly all true cases) while having very poor precision (most flagged cases are false alarms, since fever is common but the disease is rare). This drags the F-score toward zero despite the seemingly impressive recall, demonstrating why no single metric should be trusted in isolation and why metrics must be weighed jointly against the problem\'s class balance and costs of different error types.\n\n109. Many classifiers output a continuous score or probability rather than a hard label, requiring a threshold to convert scores into positive/negative predictions; changing that threshold trades recall against precision/specificity. The ROC curve plots the true positive rate (recall) against the false positive rate (1 - specificity) across all possible thresholds, and the area under the ROC curve (AUC) summarizes overall ranking quality independent of any single threshold choice. The precision-recall curve instead plots precision against recall across thresholds, which is often more informative than ROC curves when the positive class is rare, since ROC/AUC can look deceptively good under class imbalance.\n\n110. There is no universally \'best\' evaluation metric: the right choice depends on the specific domain, the problem\'s tolerance for false positives versus false negatives, the class balance in the dataset, and the algorithm being evaluated. For example, a medical screening test may prioritize recall to avoid missing true cases, while a spam filter may prioritize precision to avoid blocking legitimate messages, and these tradeoffs must be made deliberately rather than defaulting to accuracy or a single generic score.\n\n111. Classifier families: k-nearest neighbors, support vector machines, decision trees, and random forests can all be used to assign a class label to an input, but they differ in whether the decision boundary they learn is linear or non-linear. A linear classifier (like a basic SVM with a linear kernel) separates classes with a straight line, plane, or hyperplane, which works well when classes are linearly separable but fails on more complex data. Non-linear classifiers (k-NN, decision trees, random forests, kernelized SVMs) can carve out curved or piecewise decision regions, letting them fit more complex data at the cost of being more prone to overfitting and often being less interpretable. Understanding this taxonomy is the foundation for choosing an appropriate model and for appreciating why decision trees, the focus of the rest of the lecture, take a distinctly non-linear, rule-based approach to classification.\n\n112. Information content and entropy: for an event a with probability p(a), its information content (or \'surprisal\') is defined as I(a) = -log2(p(a)), measured in bits. Rare events carry high information content (learning they happened is very informative) while near-certain events carry little. Entropy H is the expected value of information content over a whole probability distribution, H = -sum(p(a) * log2(p(a))), and it measures the average uncertainty or \'impurity\' of a distribution. A uniform distribution over n outcomes maximizes entropy (log2(n) bits), while a highly skewed distribution (one outcome near-certain) has entropy near zero. The Bernoulli/coin-flip case illustrates this well: entropy as a function of p forms a symmetric curve peaking at 1 bit when p=0.5 (maximum uncertainty) and dropping to 0 bits as p approaches 0 or 1 (no uncertainty), and this curve is the standard example used to build intuition for entropy before applying it to trees.\n\n113. Building blocks of decision trees: a decision tree classifies examples by asking a sequence of questions about attributes (features), branching at each internal node based on the attribute\'s value, until reaching a leaf node that predicts a concept (class label). Trees can be simple (few nodes, shallow, easy to interpret, but possibly underfitting) or complex (many nodes, deep, capturing fine-grained patterns, but risking overfitting). The classic \'good apple\' example uses attributes like taste, color, and circumference to predict whether an apple is \'good\'; each attribute becomes a candidate splitting point, and the tree-building problem is fundamentally about choosing, at each step, which attribute best separates the examples into purer (lower-entropy) subgroups.\n\n114. The ID3 algorithm and information gain: ID3 builds a decision tree recursively and greedily by, at each node, selecting the attribute that maximizes information gain — the reduction in entropy achieved by splitting the current set of examples on that attribute. Formally, information gain for attribute A is Gain(S, A) = H(S) - sum over values v of A of (|S_v|/|S|) * H(S_v), where S is the current example set and S_v is the subset with attribute value v. The algorithm picks the attribute with the highest gain, creates a branch for each of its values, and recurses on each resulting subset until nodes are pure (single class) or no attributes remain. In the \'good apple\' example, this means comparing the information gain from splitting on taste, color, or circumference first, and building the tree top-down accordingly.\n\n115. ID3\'s bias toward many-valued attributes and the C4.5 fix: information gain systematically favors attributes with many distinct values (e.g., an ID-like attribute with a unique value per example), because splitting on such an attribute can drive subset entropy to zero even though the split is not meaningfully predictive and generalizes poorly. C4.5 corrects this by using the gain ratio instead of raw information gain: it normalizes information gain by the \'split information\' (the entropy of the attribute\'s own value distribution), penalizing attributes that split the data into many small, fragmented groups. This normalized measure favors attributes that are genuinely more discriminative rather than merely more fragmenting.\n\n116. Overfitting, overgeneralization, and pruning: decision trees grown to full depth tend to overfit — they capture noise and idiosyncrasies of the training data (overfitting) or, conversely, may miss meaningful structure if grown too shallow or with overly coarse splits (overgeneralization). Overfit trees perform well on training data but poorly on unseen data because they encode spurious patterns. Pruning addresses this by simplifying the tree after (post-pruning) or during (pre-pruning) construction — removing branches that do not improve validation performance, or that fail statistical significance tests — trading a small increase in training error for better generalization and a more interpretable, robust model.\n\n117. The bias-variance decomposition formalizes a model\'s expected prediction error as the sum of three terms: bias squared, variance, and irreducible noise. Bias measures how far the average prediction (over different training sets) is from the true function, capturing systematic error from a model being too simple to represent the underlying relationship. Variance measures how much predictions fluctuate across different training sets, capturing sensitivity to the specific data sampled. Irreducible error is noise inherent to the data-generating process that no model can eliminate. This decomposition explains the classic U-shaped test-error curve: as model complexity increases, bias tends to fall while variance rises, and the goal of model selection is to find the sweet spot that minimizes their sum.\n\n118. Single decision trees, especially when grown deep, exhibit low bias but high variance. Because a deep tree can partition the feature space into arbitrarily small regions, it can fit the training data (including its noise) almost perfectly, giving it very low bias. However, this same flexibility makes trees unstable: a small change in the training data (removing or adding a few points) can lead to a very different sequence of splits near the root, cascading into a substantially different tree structure and predictions. This instability is precisely high variance, and it is the reason trees are considered strong candidates for variance-reduction techniques rather than bias-reduction ones.\n\n119. Bagging (bootstrap aggregating) reduces variance by training many copies of a model on different bootstrap samples (random samples drawn with replacement from the training set) and averaging their predictions (or taking a majority vote for classification). Because each bootstrap sample differs slightly, each model makes somewhat different errors, and averaging cancels out much of that noise while leaving the shared, systematic signal (and bias) largely intact. Bagging is especially effective for high-variance, low-bias base learners like deep decision trees, since it directly targets the component of error where trees struggle most, without requiring any change to how each individual tree is built.\n\n120. Random forests extend bagging specifically for decision trees by adding a second source of randomness: at each split in each tree, only a random subset of features is considered as candidates, rather than all available features. This feature subsampling decorrelates the trees in the ensemble — without it, trees built on bootstrap samples of the same data tend to make similar early splits on the strongest predictors, so averaging them reduces variance less than it could. By forcing trees to sometimes ignore the dominant feature, random forests produce a more diverse set of trees whose errors are less correlated, which improves the variance-reduction benefit of averaging beyond what plain bagging achieves.\n\n121. Boosting is a complementary strategy that reduces bias by combining many weak learners (models only slightly better than random guessing) sequentially, where each new learner focuses on correcting the mistakes of the ensemble built so far. AdaBoost does this by reweighting training examples after each round: points misclassified by the current ensemble receive increased weight, forcing the next weak learner to focus more on the hard cases, and the final prediction is a weighted vote of all the weak learners. Gradient boosting generalizes this idea by fitting each new model to the residual errors (or more precisely, the negative gradient of a loss function) of the current ensemble, allowing it to optimize arbitrary differentiable loss functions; this approach underlies popular implementations like XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost.\n\n122. Despite the rise of deep learning, tree-based ensemble methods — random forests and gradient-boosted trees in particular — remain highly competitive and often state-of-the-art on structured, tabular data. They tend to require less feature engineering and hyperparameter tuning than neural networks for such data, handle mixed feature types and missing values gracefully, and are computationally efficient to train, which explains their continued dominance in industry applications and machine learning competitions involving tabular datasets.\n\n123. Classical search techniques rely on four assumptions that rarely hold in real environments: actions deterministically produce a unique next state, only the final goal is evaluated (not intermediate states), the agent always knows its exact state, and the environment does not change during execution. Understanding which assumption is violated tells you which extended technique (stochastic planning, POMDPs, replanning, etc.) is required. The Gridworld example with a drone in wind illustrates stochastic transitions: an action like \'move East\' might only succeed 80% of the time, with 10% probability of drifting north or south instead.\n\n124. An MDP is formally defined as the tuple (S, A, P, R, γ): a state space S, an action space A, a transition probability function P(s\'|s,a) giving the probability of reaching state s\' after taking action a in state s, a reward function R(s,a), and a discount factor γ ∈ [0,1]. Transition probabilities from a given state-action pair must sum to 1 across all possible successor states. The reward function is a design choice that encodes the desired agent behavior — e.g., a small negative step cost, a large positive reward at the goal, and a large penalty for an unsafe state — and changing these values changes the resulting optimal policy.\n\n125. The Markov property states that the probability of the next state depends only on the current state and action, not on the full history of prior states and actions. This means the current state must encapsulate all information relevant to predicting the future; in Gridworld the agent\'s position alone suffices, but a system with inertia (where future motion depends on past velocity, not just position) would violate the Markov property unless velocity is added to the state representation.\n\n126. Because the cumulative reward U(s0) = Σ R(sk,ak) may not converge over an infinite horizon, a discount factor γ ∈ [0,1] geometrically down-weights future rewards: U(s0) = Σ γ^k R(sk,ak). This guarantees convergence for bounded rewards and models the intuition that immediate rewards matter more than distant ones. As γ→0 the agent becomes myopic (only immediate reward matters); as γ→1 it becomes far-sighted (future rewards weighted almost as heavily as immediate ones). Since transitions are stochastic, the true optimization objective is the *expected* cumulative discounted reward, E[U(s0)], which the agent seeks to maximize via its policy.\n\n127. A policy π: S → A assigns an action to every state, in contrast to a fixed action sequence which cannot adapt to different stochastic outcomes. The optimal policy π* maximizes the expected cumulative discounted reward from every state: π* ∈ arg max_π E_π[U(s)]. For any finite MDP, there always exists a deterministic optimal policy — so randomizing actions is never strictly necessary to achieve optimality.\n\n128. The Bellman equation recursively defines the optimal value U(s) of a state as the best achievable combination of the immediate reward and the discounted expected value of the successor state, maximized over actions: U(s) = max_a [R(s,a) + γ Σ_s\' P(s\'|s,a) U(s\')]. This turns the search for an optimal policy into a system of n nonlinear equations (one per state) that cannot be solved directly in closed form because of the max operator, motivating the iterative algorithms covered in the next topic.\n\n129. Value Iteration initializes U0(s)=0 for all states and repeatedly applies the Bellman update U_{k+1}(s) ← max_a [R(s,a) + γ Σ_s\' P(s\'|s,a) U_k(s\')] until the value function stops changing (converges). In the worked Gridworld example (γ=0.9, step cost −0.04, goal +1, bad state −1), utility values propagate outward from the goal and converge after about 16 iterations; the final optimal policy is then extracted by taking the arg max action at each state using the converged utilities.\n\n130. Value Iteration converges because the Bellman update can be viewed as an operator B applied to the vector of state values, and B is a contraction mapping in the max-norm: ||B U − B U\'||∞ ≤ γ ||U − U\'||∞. Because γ < 1, repeated application of B shrinks the distance between any two value estimates by a factor of γ each iteration, so by the Banach fixed-point theorem the sequence converges to the unique fixed point of B, which is exactly the solution of the Bellman equations (the true optimal value function U*).\n\n131. Policy Iteration exploits the observation that the optimal policy typically stabilizes before the value function has fully converged numerically. It starts from an arbitrary policy π0 and alternates two steps: (1) policy evaluation, solving the *linear* system Uπ(s) = R(s,π(s)) + γ Σ_s\' P(s\'|s,π(s)) Uπ(s\') exactly for the fixed policy (no max operator, so it is a standard linear system rather than a nonlinear one); and (2) policy improvement, greedily updating the policy at every state using the just-computed value function: π\'(s) = arg max_a [R(s,a) + γ Σ_s\' P(s\'|s,a) Uπ(s\')]. The algorithm terminates when the policy stops changing, which is provably the optimal policy π*.\n\n132. Value Iteration and Policy Iteration both perform dynamic programming based on the Bellman principle of optimality (an optimal solution is built from optimal sub-solutions), but differ in what they iterate on and how. Value Iteration iterates on the value function using the Bellman max-update, usually needs many iterations, and only yields a usable policy at the very end. Policy Iteration iterates directly on policies, needs a linear-system solve plus a greedy improvement step per iteration, typically converges in far fewer iterations, and has a valid (though possibly suboptimal) policy available after every single step.\n\n133. A POMDP extends the MDP tuple (S,A,P,R,γ) with two additional components: Ω, the set of possible observations, and O(o|s), the observation model giving the probability of observing o when the true state is s. The core difficulty is that the agent never directly observes its true state s, only an observation o, and different states can produce the same observation — so the agent cannot simply apply the MDP machinery to the (unknown) true state.\n\n134. Instead of tracking an exact state, a POMDP agent maintains a belief state b(s), a probability distribution over possible true states. After taking action a and receiving observation o, the belief is updated via a Bayes filter: b\'(s\') = η · O(o|s\') · Σ_s P(s\'|s,a) b(s), where η is a normalizing constant. This update has two conceptual steps: prediction (propagate the old belief through the transition model P) and filtering/correction (reweight the predicted belief by how well each candidate state s\' explains the new observation via O(o|s\')).\n\n135. A POMDP can be reformulated as an MDP defined over belief states rather than raw states: the belief plays the role of the state, the Bayes update plays the role of the transition function, the expected reward Σ_s b(s)R(s,a) plays the role of the reward, and the policy maps beliefs to actions (π: b → a) instead of states to actions. This yields an analogous Bellman equation over beliefs: U(b) = max_a [Σ_s b(s)R(s,a) + γ Σ_o P(o|b,a) U(b\'_{a,o})]. The key added difficulty compared to standard MDPs is that the belief space is continuous (an infinite state space), so exact solutions are rarely practical and approximation methods are required.\n\n136. When ML systems make wrong predictions, the consequences range from irrelevant (an ad recommendation) to critical (a treatment recommendation). In medicine and other physical-world applications, models must communicate their uncertainty so that users can distinguish reliable from unreliable predictions — separating \'known unknowns\' (quantifiable uncertainty) from \'unknown unknowns.\' A concrete failure mode: a skin-lesion classifier trained on one hospital\'s images can confidently (99% confidence) misclassify a melanoma as benign when applied to images from a different hospital/population due to data drift — demonstrating that standard networks are not calibrated or reliable and cannot distinguish their own correct from incorrect high-confidence predictions.\n\n137. Calibration formalizes the idea that predicted class scores should represent the true likelihood of the underlying event. Canonical calibration is the strongest and most general form: the full model output f(X) should equal the true conditional distribution of the label, f(X) = P(Y|f(X)). Top-label calibration is the weakest, most commonly used form: only the confidence of the predicted (arg max) class must match its true correctness probability, f_K(X) = P(Y=K|f_K(X)) where K=argmax_k f_k(X). There is a spectrum of calibration definitions between these extremes, and choosing which one to target is itself a design decision that affects downstream conclusions about trustworthiness.\n\n138. Because the true conditional distribution P(Y|f(X)) is unknown and only one label sample exists per prediction, calibration error must be estimated, typically via binning (Expected Calibration Error, ECE: split predictions into confidence bins, compare mean confidence vs. mean accuracy per bin, and take a weighted average of the gaps) or kernel density estimation (KDE). In practice, computing a calibration error requires three choices: the calibration definition (canonical vs. top-label), the distance function, and the estimator. Crucially, commonly used calibration estimators are biased and their bias scales with dataset size — shown empirically to affect CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet benchmarks — so calibration numbers must be interpreted with this bias in mind, especially when comparing across models or datasets of different sizes.\n\n139. Post-hoc calibration adjusts a trained model\'s output probabilities after training, without changing its weights or architecture, and typically without affecting accuracy. The most common technique is temperature scaling: divide the logits z by a single learned scalar temperature T before the softmax, Q̂ = max_c softmax(z/T)(c), with T fit on an independent validation set (T=1 is no change, T>1 softens/reduces overconfidence, T<1 sharpens confidence). Extensions include accounting for distribution shift via generic perturbations during calibration, and learning prediction-specific/adaptive temperatures rather than one global value. In practice, post-hoc calibrated models should always be re-validated by computing calibration error on an independent test set, since calibration quality is application-dependent and what works for natural-image benchmarks may not transfer to specialized domains like medical imaging.\n\n140. Uncertainty in a prediction reflects either randomness or a lack of knowledge about the target variable. Aleatoric uncertainty is irreducible randomness inherent to the data-generating process — it cannot be reduced even with unlimited data. Epistemic uncertainty stems from limited data or knowledge and can, in principle, be reduced by collecting more/better data; it should be high in low-data regions and for out-of-distribution inputs. Quantifying epistemic uncertainty remains an open research question: one principled approach is via a bias-variance decomposition of the model\'s loss; commonly used practical alternatives include distributional approaches (e.g., SWAG, Evidential Deep Learning), Deep Ensembles, MC Dropout, and other specialized deterministic methods.\n\n141. Two widely used practical methods for estimating epistemic uncertainty are MC Dropout and Deep Ensembles. MC Dropout keeps dropout active at inference time and performs multiple stochastic forward passes through a single trained network, using the variance across passes as an uncertainty estimate (theoretically justified as approximating a deep Gaussian process). Deep Ensembles instead train several independently initialized copies of the same architecture and use the disagreement/variance across their predictions as the uncertainty estimate. Deep Ensembles generally give more reliable, better-decorrelated uncertainty estimates than MC Dropout (which tends to underestimate uncertainty, especially out-of-distribution), but at higher computational cost since multiple full networks must be trained and stored.\n\n142. Bregman Information (BI) is a method for quantifying epistemic uncertainty about the data-generating process directly in logit space. It is computed via test-time augmentation (TTA): generate P augmented versions of an input, collect their logit vectors ẑ_i, and compute u(x)_BI = (1/P)Σ LSE(ẑ_i) − LSE((1/P)Σ ẑ_i), where LSE is the log-sum-exp function. Unlike plain softmax confidence, BI has been shown to be robust to distribution shifts — remaining a reliable epistemic-uncertainty signal even when a model\'s raw confidence becomes miscalibrated under data drift. Epistemic uncertainty estimates like BI are especially useful for active learning/testing, continual/lifelong learning, detecting distribution shift, interactive settings, and triaging cases for human review.\n\n143. Uncertainty quantification extends naturally to generative models and large language models (LLMs), where the goal is often to detect hallucinations. A basic approach: ask the model the same question multiple times, embed the answers in a vector space, and compute a generalized variance of the answers as an uncertainty signal (motivated by the bias-variance decomposition of the kernel scoring rule). A more refined \'spectral uncertainty\' approach goes beyond surface-level hallucination detection by handling ambiguous questions: an auxiliary LLM generates several clarifications of the original (possibly ambiguous) question (outer samples), the target LLM generates several answers per clarification (inner samples), the answers are embedded, kernel matrices are built per-clarification and overall, and the von Neumann entropy (VNE) of these kernel matrices — e.g., SpecE(Clarification_i) = −Tr[K_i log K_i] — is used as the uncertainty measure, capturing uncertainty due to ambiguity as well as randomness in generation.\n\n144. Classical machine learning trains one model per narrowly defined task, requiring separate feature engineering and datasets for each. Deep learning shifted this by letting networks learn their own representations from raw data. Foundation models go further: a single, very large model is pretrained (usually self-supervised) on broad, diverse data, and this one model can then be adapted — via fine-tuning or prompting — to a wide variety of downstream tasks, rather than being built from scratch each time.\n\nThis shift is accompanied by two key phenomena: emergence, where qualitatively new capabilities appear as models are scaled up (capabilities not present, or not predictable, in smaller models), and homogenization, where the field converges on a small number of underlying architectures (predominantly Transformers) used across many different applications and modalities.\n\n145. Segment Anything (SAM) is a foundation model for computer vision (not language), developed by Meta AI. Earlier vision segmentation used two separate approaches: interactive, human-guided segmentation and automatic, object-specific segmentation. SAM unifies this into a single promptable model — it can be prompted with points, boxes, masks, or text — trained on the SA-1B dataset containing about 1 billion masks, allowing it to generalize (zero-shot) to new domains and tasks it wasn\'t explicitly trained for. Its successor, SAM 2 (2024), extends the same promptable-segmentation idea to video, enabling object tracking across frames.\n\nSAM is a good example of the foundation model paradigm applied outside of language: one broadly pretrained model, adaptable via prompting, replacing many narrow, task-specific vision models.\n\n146. A language model (LM) is formally a probability distribution p(x1, x2, ..., xL) over sequences of tokens, where a token can be a word, a word-piece (subword), or even a single character (including punctuation/symbols). The LM assigns a plausibility score to any candidate sequence: grammatically and semantically sensible sequences should receive higher probability than nonsensical ones.\n\nFor example, given the vocabulary {the, mouse, ate, cheese, ball}, a good LM assigns p("the mouse ate the cheese") = 0.02, much higher than p("the cheese ate the mouse") = 0.01 (grammatical but implausible: mice eat cheese, not vice versa) and far higher than p("mouse the the cheese ate") = 0.0001 (ungrammatical). This shows the LM combines syntactic knowledge (word order/grammar) with world knowledge (mice eat cheese).\n\n147. Since directly modeling the joint probability of a whole sequence is intractable, LMs factorize it using the chain rule of probability: p(x1:L) = p(x1) · p(x2|x1) · p(x3|x1,x2) · ... = ∏_{i=1}^{L} p(xi | x1:i-1). Each conditional distribution p(xi | x1:i-1) — the probability of the next token given everything before it — is computed by a neural network.\n\nThis is exactly what makes modern LLMs autoregressive: the model never computes the full joint probability in one shot, but builds it up token by token, each time conditioning on all previously generated (or given) tokens. During training this is done in parallel via teacher forcing (using ground-truth previous tokens); during inference/generation it must proceed sequentially, one token at a time.\n\n148. To generate text, you provide a prefix (the prompt) and then sample the remaining tokens one at a time: for i = 1, ..., L: xi ~ p(xi | x1:i-1)^(1/T). The temperature T rescales the model\'s probability distribution before sampling and controls the randomness of generation.\n\nAt T=0, generation is deterministic — always the single most probable token is chosen (argmax). At T=1, tokens are sampled exactly according to the model\'s own learned distribution. As T→∞, the distribution flattens toward uniform, giving maximal randomness. In practice, higher temperature produces more creative/diverse text but also increases the risk of incoherent or factually wrong output; lower temperature produces more focused, conservative, repetitive text. Related sampling controls used in practice include top-k (restrict to the k most likely tokens) and top-p/nucleus sampling (restrict to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds a threshold p).\n\n149. Before 2017, sequence models like RNNs processed tokens step by step, which was slow (no parallelism across time steps) and prone to "forgetting" information from far back in the sequence (vanishing gradients / limited effective memory). The 2017 paper "Attention Is All You Need" introduced the Transformer, which processes the entire sequence at once rather than step by step.\n\nThe Transformer consists of a stack of encoder blocks and a stack of decoder blocks. Each encoder block contains a self-attention sub-layer followed by a position-wise feed-forward network (with residual connections and normalization, though not emphasized in this lecture\'s slides). Attention lets every token directly form a weighted combination of every other token\'s information, regardless of distance, and this can be computed in parallel across the whole sequence — a major reason Transformers train efficiently on GPUs.\n\n150. Self-attention is the mechanism by which each token in a sequence gathers relevant context from every other token. Intuitively, for a word like "it" in a sentence, self-attention lets the model figure out which earlier word (e.g. "the animal") it refers to — resolving references and dependencies without any hand-coded grammar rules. This behavior is entirely learned from data during training, not programmed explicitly.\n\nThis is the key building block that replaces recurrence: instead of passing a hidden state sequentially, every token can attend to (look at, and weight) every other token\'s representation directly.\n\n151. Self-attention computes three vectors from each token embedding x via learned weight matrices: query q = xW_Q, key k = xW_K, and value v = xW_V. These play the role familiar from information retrieval: the query represents "what am I looking for", the key represents "what do I match against" (each token advertises itself via its key), and the value represents "what content is actually returned" if there\'s a match.\n\nEvery token produces its own q, k, and v; a token\'s query is compared against every other token\'s key to determine how much attention to pay to that token\'s value.\n\n152. For a given query qi, the raw attention score against another token\'s key kj is the dot product: score_ij = qi · kj. This score is then scaled by 1/√d_k (where d_k is the key vector dimension) before applying softmax — this scaling prevents the dot products from growing too large in magnitude, which would push softmax into regions with extremely small gradients and destabilize training.\n\nAfter scaling, softmax converts the scores into a set of positive weights summing to 1 across all tokens being attended to — these are the attention weights.\n\n153. The final self-attention output for a token, zi, is the weighted sum of all value vectors, weighted by the softmax attention weights: zi = Σ_j softmax_j · vj. Every token gets its own contextualized output vector zi that blends information from all other tokens according to how much attention it pays to each. Crucially, this computation happens simultaneously for every position in the sequence — it is highly parallelizable, unlike the sequential processing in RNNs.\n\nIn compact matrix form, stacking all token embeddings as rows of a matrix X and computing Q = XW_Q, K = XW_K, V = XW_V, the entire operation is: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^T / √d_k) · V — just a handful of matrix multiplications, which is why Transformers run efficiently on GPUs.\n\n154. Self-attention on its own is permutation-invariant — it would treat "the animal bit the man" the same as "the man bit the animal" if word order weren\'t encoded somehow, since attention just computes weighted sums regardless of position. To fix this, a positional vector ti is added to each token embedding before attention, injecting information about each token\'s position in the sequence.\n\nThe original Transformer used fixed sinusoidal positional encodings. Modern LLMs mostly use RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding), which rotates query/key vectors in a way that encodes relative position directly into the attention computation and generalizes better to sequence lengths not seen during training.\n\n155. Instead of computing attention once, Transformers use multi-head attention: several attention "heads" run in parallel, each with its own independently learned W_Q, W_K, W_V matrices. Because each head is initialized and trained separately, different heads tend to specialize in learning different kinds of relationships between tokens — e.g., one head might focus on syntactic structure, another on coreference (which pronoun refers to which noun), another on part-of-speech patterns.\n\nThe outputs of all heads are concatenated and passed through a linear projection to produce the final attention output. In practice, models typically use 8–16 heads per attention layer. Running one attention computation would force all these different relationship types to be averaged together into a single representation; multiple heads let the model represent several relationship types simultaneously.\n\n156. After the final decoder block produces an output vector, a linear layer maps this vector to a single logit value per vocabulary entry (one score for every possible next token). Softmax then converts these logits into a probability distribution over the vocabulary. The next token is chosen either via argmax (always the highest-probability token, deterministic) or via sampling (introducing randomness, as controlled by temperature).\n\nAfter a token is chosen, it\'s appended to the sequence and the whole process repeats to generate the following token — this is what makes generation autoregressive: each new token is generated conditioned on all tokens generated so far.\n\n157. Pretraining trains a very large neural network on massive amounts of unlabeled text scraped from the internet (Wikipedia, Reddit, books, code, etc.), with the sole training objective of predicting the next word/token given the preceding context. This requires billions of parameters and enormous compute budgets (GPU-years, millions of dollars). The result is a base model (e.g. GPT-3 in 2020) that has absorbed a huge amount of linguistic and world knowledge simply by learning to compress and predict text.\n\nHowever, a base model only continues text plausibly — it does not inherently follow instructions or behave like an assistant; given a question, it might just continue with more questions rather than answering. Two further training stages (instruction fine-tuning and RLHF) are needed to turn it into something like a chatbot.\n\n158. Instruction fine-tuning takes the pretrained base model and continues training it, in a supervised way, on many pairs of (instruction, good answer) — the model learns to imitate the desired response style for a given instruction. Example datasets include Alpaca (52k instruction-response pairs, generated via self-instruct) and Baize (dialogue data). A worked example: instruction "Explain photosynthesis in one sentence" paired with the answer "Plants convert light, water, and CO2 into sugar and oxygen."\n\nAfter this stage the model reliably follows instructions rather than just continuing text arbitrarily. However, this alone doesn\'t resolve which of several plausible-sounding answers is actually better — that\'s the problem RLHF addresses next.\n\n159. RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) is the third training stage. First, humans compare and rank several candidate model responses to the same prompt by quality. Second, this ranking data is used to train a separate reward model rθ that learns to predict a scalar "quality" score for any given response — high for responses humans preferred, low otherwise. Third, the language model itself is fine-tuned using reinforcement learning to maximize the score given by rθ.\n\nFraming this as an RL problem: the policy is the language model, the action is choosing the next token, and the reward comes from the reward model\'s score of the full generated response. A KL-divergence penalty term is added to keep the fine-tuned model from drifting too far from the original base/instruction-tuned model (preventing reward hacking and degenerate outputs). A popular modern alternative is DPO (Direct Preference Optimization), which optimizes directly on preference pairs without needing to train a separate reward model or run full RL.\n\n160. Fine-tuning all billions of parameters of a large model for every new task is computationally expensive and memory-intensive. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is based on the observation that the necessary weight update ΔW during adaptation tends to have low intrinsic rank — it can be well approximated by the product of two much smaller matrices, ΔW = A·B^T, where A and B have far fewer parameters than the full weight matrix W.\n\nDuring LoRA fine-tuning, the original pretrained weight matrix W is frozen (not updated); only the small A and B matrices are trained. This drastically reduces the number of trainable parameters and the memory needed for fine-tuning, while achieving performance close to full fine-tuning on many tasks. LoRA is also widely used beyond language models, e.g., to adapt text-to-image diffusion models to new styles.\n\n161. An early milestone in multimodal foundation models was Flamingo (DeepMind, 2022), which combined a frozen large language model with visual inputs via learnable adapters, accepting interleaved text and images. As of 2026, frontier models (e.g. GPT-5, Gemini 3, Claude Opus 4.x) have moved beyond bolting a vision encoder onto a language model: they are natively multimodal, meaning a single Transformer architecture is trained end-to-end to handle text, images, audio, and video together, without a separate dedicated image encoder module.\n\n162. Diffusion is the other major breakthrough behind modern generative image models. During training, noise is progressively added to real images (the forward process) until nothing but pure random noise remains; a neural network is then trained to reverse this process, gradually removing noise step by step to recover a clean image. Generation starts from pure noise and repeatedly applies the learned denoising network to arrive at a realistic image.\n\nFor text-to-image generation (e.g. DALL·E-3, Stable Diffusion 3, FLUX, Imagen 3, Midjourney), a Transformer-based text encoder first encodes the input prompt, and this text representation conditions the diffusion process so that the generated image matches the description.\n\n163. LLM agents extend a language model from single-turn question answering into multi-step planning and acting using external tools — searching the web, executing code, calling APIs, controlling a computer ("computer-use" agents), or writing and running code ("coding agents"). Games serve as convenient testbeds for agent research: Voyager (2023) had an LLM play and progress through Minecraft autonomously, and DeepMind\'s SIMA is a generalist game agent; earlier, purely RL-based approaches like VPT (2022) tackled Minecraft without an LLM component.\n\nConceptually, agents combine planning (deciding what sequence of actions/tool calls to take) with reinforcement learning ideas (acting in an environment, gathering feedback) — connecting back to the RL/MDP framework covered in the RLHF context.\n\n164. Progress in LLMs is heavily industry-driven, and many state-of-the-art models are closed-source, which makes independent reproducibility and evaluation difficult; open models (Llama, DeepSeek, Qwen, Mistral) have however substantially closed the gap with closed models. Training these models is extremely energy-intensive: GPT-3 (2020, 175 billion parameters) was estimated to produce around 550 tons of CO2 during training, and newer, larger models consume even more. This raises the broader philosophical question of whether an LLM is genuinely "intelligent" (the strong vs. weak AI debate).\n\nBeyond compute costs, generative models raise concrete risk categories: short-term risks include hallucination (confidently generating false information), susceptibility to prompt injection attacks, and being manipulated into producing toxic content; longer-term risks fall under "alignment" — the worry that increasingly capable AI systems might pursue goals misaligned with human intentions, feeding into broader debates about AI race dynamics and loss of human control. Separately, since generative models are trained on internet-scale data, they can reproduce and amplify societal biases and stereotypes present in that data, and raise unresolved questions about data consent and copyright.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 165 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array.'
