2026-09-13 18:13:01,740 INFO fatass: free(): cwd=C:\Users\Qi\Documents\GitHub\fatass\home\courses\prototype\lectures add_dirs=[] silent=True permission_mode=bypassPermissions model=sonnet effort=None tools=Read,Write prompt="Below are numbered overview texts for individual learning 'points' from one lecture's notes. For EACH numbered overview, write a short, specific title (roughly 3-8 words) that names what that point is actually about — a proper name/label, NOT a restatement or truncation of the opening sentence. For example, for an overview beginning 'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule: the main lecture meets every Thursday...', a good title is 'Weekly Lecture and Exercise Schedule', not 'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule'.\n\n0. The stated goal of AI is to build an intelligent agent - ideally one that is autonomous, learning, intelligent, and automatic. Russell & Norvig's influential 2x2 classification splits AI approaches along two axes: thinking vs. acting, and humanly vs. rationally. This yields four schools: think humanly (cognitive science, model how humans actually reason), think rationally (the logicist approach using formal logic and deduction), act humanly (behavioral equivalence to a human, epitomized by the Turing Test), and act rationally (build agents that maximize expected outcome). The lecture argues the rational-agent approach is the most general, since it does not require human-likeness as a precondition and can still incorporate the other approaches when they happen to be rational. This is also the approach the course itself follows: an engineering-oriented focus on directly programmable, especially machine-learning-based, AI methods.\n\n1. AI research splits into two broad technical paradigms. The symbolic / physical-symbol-system paradigm relies on explicit programming, logics, rules, and inference procedures - strong for drawing verifiable conclusions, interpretability, and giving mathematical guarantees, but brittle and labor-intensive to hand-engineer. The connectionist paradigm relies on artificial neural networks that learn distributed representations directly from data - strong for perception, pattern recognition, and handling noisy or high-dimensional data, but harder to interpret ('black box'). The lecture stresses that complex modern AI systems (AlphaFold, Aristotle, LLM agents) typically combine both paradigms rather than choosing one exclusively. In large language models specifically, the classical separation between 'knowledge representation' and 'inference mechanism' (the Smith knowledge-representation hypothesis) breaks down, since both are fused into the same trained network.\n\n2. The lecture highlights several modern AI systems as case studies of combined-paradigm approaches: AlphaGo (2016) beat world champion Lee Sedol at Go using Monte Carlo Tree Search plus neural network position evaluation; AlphaFold solved the decades-old protein-folding problem by combining search with deep learning, earning the Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2024; and Aristotle (Harmonic, 2025) combined Monte Carlo Graph Search, reinforcement learning, LLM-based informal reasoning, and the Lean formal-proof checker to win an IMO-2025 gold-medal-equivalent result (5 of 6 problems, all machine-verified). The lecture also surveys the standard benchmark landscape used to measure progress: MMLU, GPQA Diamond, ARC-AGI-2, Humanity's Last Exam, SWE-bench, Chatbot Arena, and AgentBench/tau-bench - while cautioning with Goodhart's Law that 'when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure': models can be over-optimized for benchmark scores without a corresponding real capability gain.\n\n3. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting, introduced by Wei et al. (2022), instructs a large language model to lay out its reasoning process step by step before producing a final answer, rather than jumping directly from question to answer. Standard prompting treats the model as a black box (direct question, direct answer, no visible reasoning), whereas CoT prompting makes the intermediate reasoning steps explicit and traceable. The lecture's canonical example: asking how much Peter spends buying apples and oranges - standard prompting just outputs the total, while CoT prompting shows the multiplication and addition steps explicitly, which tends to produce more accurate results on complex, multi-step problems.\n\n4. There are two main ways to induce CoT reasoning. Zero-shot CoT simply appends a trigger phrase such as 'Let's think step by step' to the prompt, with no worked examples - this is surprisingly effective, but only once a model exceeds a certain scale (an emergent capability that doesn't appear in smaller models). Few-shot CoT instead demonstrates 2-8 example question/reasoning/answer triples before the actual question, so the model imitates the pattern of step-by-step reasoning; this tends to be especially effective on complex reasoning tasks such as arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning.\n\n5. The lecture raises the question of whether CoT reveals genuine 'thinking' or merely a simulated reasoning trace. Human thinking involves consciousness, intentionality, and grounded experience of meaning; an LLM instead predicts the next token based on statistical patterns learned from training data, with no consciousness or genuine world-knowledge. The lecture frames this as 'simulated' reproduction of reasoning patterns from training data, versus something 'novel': functional equivalence, abstraction, and transfer. Practically, regardless of the metaphysical answer, CoT provides real benefits: better accuracy on logical tasks, fewer hallucinations, and more explainable/traceable decisions (relevant to Explainable AI).\n\n6. Materialism holds that nothing exists except matter - mind, thought, and will all arise from matter. Its consequence for AI: since everything that makes up a human, including intelligence and thought, can in principle be studied and analyzed by natural science, everything is in principle constructible, including a thinking, intelligent human-equivalent system. This underwrites the claim that AI is possible in principle.\n\n7. Behaviorism holds that only observable behavior is a valid subject of science; unverifiable or unfalsifiable questions (like 'does it really believe/want/fear something') are meaningless, since beliefs, ideas, and consciousness are just descriptions of behavior patterns. Consequence: two systems (e.g., a human and a machine) are equivalent if they exhibit the same input/output behavior - real internal understanding is irrelevant to the behaviorist. This grounds the 'weak AI hypothesis': a system can act as if intelligent without needing to actually be conscious.\n\n8. Functionalism holds that what matters is not what a system is made of but what it does - mental states are defined by their functional/causal role (relations between inputs, other mental states, and behavioral outputs), not by their physical substrate. The lecture's example: DAB (digital) and FM (analog) radios are technically completely different but functionally equivalent (both turn a signal into music); by contrast, streaming and radio are not functionally equivalent. Formally, system A in S1 is functionally equivalent to B in S2 if identical inputs produce identical outputs and functionally equivalent successor states - implying that a brain (neurons) and a computer (transistors) could in principle be functionally equivalent. Materialism plus functionalism together support the 'strong AI hypothesis': that a functionally equivalent machine would really think and be conscious, not just simulate thinking, as opposed to the weak AI hypothesis (behaviorism-supported) that machines merely act as if intelligent.\n\n9. Proposed by Alan Turing as a test for the strong AI hypothesis: a human questioner communicates via text with a hidden human and a hidden computer, without knowing which is which, and tries to identify the computer. If the questioner cannot reliably distinguish computer from human, the test is passed. Criticisms include that the test's outcome depends heavily on the questioner's own skill and knowledge (not objective), and that it can be gamed by systems built as giant lookup tables of canned responses without any real intelligence (as ELIZA demonstrated). The 'Total Turing Test' extends the classic version with video transmission and object recognition to test embodied perception as well as language.\n\n10. Searle's 1980 Chinese Room argument: a person who understands no Chinese sits in a room, receives Chinese characters through a slot, and uses a rulebook (in their own language) to produce grammatically correct Chinese responses, fooling outside observers into thinking a Chinese speaker is inside. Searle's conclusion: the person is manipulating pure syntax without understanding any semantics or meaning, so no part of the system truly 'understands' Chinese - this is presented as a direct argument against the strong AI hypothesis, i.e. symbol manipulation alone cannot produce genuine understanding. The behaviorist counter-argument: the whole system (person + rulebook + room) does understand, since understanding is observable in its correct outputs, even if no individual component does.\n\n11. The lecture also discusses a set of alternative tests for evaluating whether an AI system is approaching AGI: the coffee-preparation test (a robot must make coffee in an arbitrary kitchen), the IKEA test (assemble a disassembled, packaged IKEA piece of furniture), and the student test (pass standardized human exams such as bar exams or the SAT). The ARC Prize / ARC-AGI benchmark is presented as a rigorous modern AGI benchmark: a $1M+ open-source competition targeting the ARC-AGI benchmark, which measures generalization ability on genuinely novel tasks (not memorizable from training data) via abstraction and reasoning. ARC-AGI-1 targeted basic fluid intelligence, ARC-AGI-2 emphasizes adaptability and efficiency; about 98.7% of ARC tasks are human-solvable, underscoring a persistent gap between human and artificial general intelligence, though note that even ARC-AGI has shown susceptibility to Goodhart's-Law-style benchmark gaming as scores rose quickly.\n\n12. An agent is anything that perceives its environment through sensors and acts upon it through actuators. Formally, the agent function maps the sequence of percepts (observations) received so far to an action; this function can be implemented by an agent program. The lecture generalizes this with additional inputs beyond raw observation: prior knowledge (e.g., a map), learned/experiential knowledge (e.g., test cases), and goals/priorities, all feeding into the choice of the next action via reasoning or learning.\n\n13. A rational (intelligent) agent is defined as one that always chooses the optimal action with respect to a performance measure, given its percept sequence and prior knowledge of the environment. Choosing the right performance measure is itself a nontrivial design task, illustrated with Russell & Norvig's vacuum-world example: a two-square world where the agent can move, suck up dirt, or do nothing, and different performance measures (e.g., 'always maximally clean' vs. 'clean but minimize power use' vs. 'clean but minimize disturbance') lead to different notions of what counts as the optimal, rational agent.\n\n14. Task environments are classified along several dimensions that determine how hard the agent's job is: fully observable vs. partially observable (does the sensor data give complete state information, e.g. a vacuum agent that can only see its own square); deterministic vs. stochastic (does the next state follow with certainty from the current state and action, e.g. dirt appearing randomly); episodic vs. sequential (are episodes independent atomic events, or do current actions affect the future, e.g. chess); static vs. dynamic or semi-dynamic (can the environment change while the agent is still deliberating, e.g. chess with a clock); discrete vs. continuous; and single-agent vs. multi-agent (further split into competitive/adversarial vs. cooperative). The lecture works through Russell & Norvig's classic table of example environments (crossword puzzles, chess with a clock, poker, backgammon, taxi driving, medical diagnosis, image analysis, an interactive English tutor) classified along these axes.\n\n15. Classical agent architectures form a hierarchy of increasing sophistication and decreasing reliance on explicit hand-programming: the simple reflex agent maps observation directly to action via a lookup table or condition-action rules with no memory; the model-based reflex agent maintains internal state to track aspects of the world it cannot currently observe; the goal-based agent chooses actions that move it toward an explicit goal and can recognize goal achievement; the utility-based agent uses a quantitative utility function to rank actions, allowing it to handle trade-offs between competing objectives; and the learning agent adds a learning element (plus critic and exploration element) that lets the agent improve its behavior from experience, either online during operation or offline during a training phase. The lecture connects this to LLM-based Foundation Agents, where the actual 'learning' mostly happens during pretraining, and runtime behavior is instead adapted via prompts, tools, and refinement loops.\n\n16. Foundation models, per the Stanford CRFM (2021) definition, are large models pretrained (usually self-supervised, often multimodally across text, code, image, audio, video) on broad data that serve as a base for many downstream applications via transfer at scale, with parameter counts reaching up to roughly 10^12. Scale itself produces emergent abilities - capabilities that appear abruptly once a critical model size is crossed and were neither specifically trained for nor easy to predict in advance, such as chain-of-thought reasoning, few-shot learning, and instruction-following (e.g., reliable multi-digit addition emerging only around ~13B parameters).\n\n17. Unlike classical agents that map observation directly to action, a Foundation Agent wraps an LLM core with modular extensions: memory/context, tool-use, planning, and reasoning. A key architectural difference from classical agents is the absence of a strict separation between knowledge representation and inference mechanism. Tool-Using is the capability that lets these agents call external systems - search engines and RAG for information retrieval, Python interpreters or simulators for computation, databases/cloud services/browsers for system interaction, and specialized models for image/audio analysis or translation - extending the model beyond what it can do from its weights alone; a key rule of thumb from the lecture is that a tool-using agent is only as capable as the tools it has access to. The ReAct paradigm ('Reasoning + Acting') operationalizes this by interleaving explicit reasoning steps with tool-call actions and observations in a loop (e.g., reason about what info is needed, call a search tool, observe result, reason further), which the lecture illustrates with a worked example comparing Grand Slam titles of two tennis players.\n\n18. Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) consist of multiple interacting autonomous agents that jointly solve complex problems by cooperating, competing, or coordinating. Classical (pre-Foundation-Model) MAS relied on limited, structured agent communication and explicit coordination protocols with high development effort, whereas modern LLM-based MAS use flexible natural-language communication and can show emergent coordination with faster development. Benefits include division of labor, specialization, redundancy/robustness, scalability, and emergent collective intelligence exceeding the sum of individual agents. A key technique is Multi-Agent Debate, where agents (e.g., a Proposer and a Critic