xioms and inference rules can be viewed as searching through the space of statements derivable from the axioms, applying inference rules as 'moves' until the target statement (the goal) is reached. Here the search space is often huge or only implicitly defined — rather than being handed all possible derivations, the search procedure generates new candidate statements on the fly, which mirrors the general theme that the full search space is rarely known in advance.\n\n3. Search in agents and planning: an agent choosing its next action can be understood as searching over possible actions for the one that maximizes some notion of value or reward, while planning extends this to searching over sequences of actions that transform an initial situation into one satisfying a goal condition. Planning problems highlight the added complexity of chaining decisions together, since the value of one action depends on which actions follow it, requiring search over multi-step trajectories rather than single choices.\n\n4. Search in optimization: finding the maximum (or minimum) of a function is itself a search problem — the 'state space' is the function's domain, and the search procedure (e.g., hill climbing, gradient-based methods, simulated annealing) navigates that domain looking for a state (input) that best satisfies the goal criterion (highest function value). This reframes numerical optimization as structurally identical to the other search problems, differing mainly in whether the space is discrete or continuous and whether gradient information is available.\n\n5. Search in LLMs: generating text with a large language model can be viewed as searching over the space of possible next tokens (or full answers) for ones that best satisfy an implicit goal, such as high likelihood or alignment with a prompt's intent. Techniques like greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling are all search strategies over this token space, and more elaborate approaches (e.g., chain-of-thought or tree-of-thought prompting) explicitly search over multiple candidate reasoning paths before settling on an answer — showing that even modern neural methods inherit the same initial-state-to-goal-state search structure seen elsewhere in AI.\n\n6. A search problem is formally defined by three ingredients: an initial state (the starting point of the search), a successor function N(·) that, given a state, returns the set of possible next states (often along with the action and cost associated with each transition), and a goal test that determines whether a given state counts as a solution. This triple is enough to specify any search problem abstractly, independent of the domain it comes from, which is what makes the framework so widely reusable across chess, theorem proving, route planning, and more.\n\n7. Unlike a graph algorithm that operates on an explicit adjacency list, most real search problems have graphs that are far too large (or infinite) to write down in advance. Instead, the graph is defined implicitly: nodes and edges are generated on demand by calling the successor function only on states actually visited during the search. This 'online' expansion is what allows search to scale to enormous or unbounded state spaces, such as all possible chess positions or all possible token sequences a language model could generate.\n\n8. The abstract search framework applies to very different-looking problems once you identify the right initial state, successor function, and goal test. In chess, a state is a board position, successors are legal moves, and the goal test checks for checkmate. In deduction systems, a state is a set of known facts or a partial proof, successors apply inference rules, and the goal test checks whether the target statement has been derived. In route planning, a state is a location, successors are reachable neighboring locations (with edge costs like distance or time), and the goal is reaching the destination. Recognizing this shared structure lets the same search algorithms be reused across domains.\n\n9. LLM decoding can itself be cast as a search problem: the initial state is the prompt (the sequence of tokens generated so far), the successor function proposes candidate next tokens weighted by the model's predicted probability distribution, and the goal test is satisfied when an end-of-sequence token is produced or a maximum length is reached. The path cost of a generated sequence is defined as the negative log-probability of the tokens along that path, so lower cost corresponds to higher-probability (more likely) sequences. This reframing lets classical search algorithms and their trade-offs be applied directly to the problem of generating text.\n\n10. Greedy decoding behaves like depth-first search: at each step it commits to the single next token with the highest probability and never reconsiders that choice, following one path deeply without backtracking. This makes it fast and cheap since only one hypothesis is tracked at a time, but it is risky because an early locally-optimal token choice can lock the search into a globally suboptimal or low-quality sequence, with no way to recover.\n\n11. Beam search behaves more like breadth-first search: instead of keeping only one hypothesis, it maintains a fixed number k (the beam width) of the most promising partial sequences at each step, expanding all of them and pruning down to the top k by cumulative path cost (negative log-probability) after each expansion. This explores more of the search space than greedy decoding and typically produces better-scoring sequences, but at a proportionally higher computational cost since k hypotheses must be expanded and scored at every step instead of just one.\n\n12. The n-Queens problem asks how to place n queens on an n×n chessboard so that no two queens attack each other (no shared row, column, or diagonal). While the goal itself is simple to state, how one chooses to represent a 'state' of the search — a partial or complete arrangement of queens — is not fixed by the problem statement. The lecture uses n-Queens precisely because it is simple enough to reason about by hand, yet rich enough to expose how dramatically the size of the search space can change depending on modeling choices, making it an ideal vehicle for teaching state-space design.\n\n13. The most naive representation treats a state as an arbitrary subset of the board's n^2 squares, where each square is either occupied by a queen or empty, with no built-in constraints on how many queens are placed or where. This yields 2^(n^2) possible states, since each of the n^2 squares independently can be 'queen' or 'no queen'. For n=8 this is 2^64, an astronomically large number that makes brute-force search over this state space computationally infeasible, even though the actual solutions we care about occupy a tiny corner of this space.\n\n14. A smarter representation exploits the fact that any valid solution must have exactly one queen per column (since two queens sharing a column always attack each other). By restricting the state space to only those configurations with exactly one queen per column, each of the n columns independently chooses one of n possible rows, giving n^n total states. For n=8 this is 8^8 (about 16.7 million), a massive reduction from 2^64, illustrating how encoding a known constraint directly into the representation — rather than leaving it to be discovered during search — shrinks the space by many orders of magnitude.\n\n15. An even tighter representation additionally exploits the fact that no two queens can share a row either, so a valid solution must assign each column a distinct row — i.e., a permutation of the row indices 1..n. Representing states as permutations (bijective column-to-row mappings) reduces the state space to n! (8! = 40,320 for n=8), factoring out not just the column constraint but also the row constraint. This is dramatically smaller than both the naive (2^64) and one-queen-per-column (8^8) representations, though diagonal-attack constraints still must be checked during search since they are not encoded into the representation itself.\n\n16. The overarching lesson is that the choice of state-space representation is often more decisive for tractability than the choice of search algorithm. By building known problem constraints (one queen per column, one queen per row) directly into what counts as a 'state,' the search space shrinks from 2^(n^2) to n^n to n!, illustrating a general principle in AI and algorithm design: a well-chosen representation can turn an intractable brute-force problem into a practically solvable one, and modeling decisions should be considered as carefully as the search or optimization procedure applied afterward.\n\n17. A search graph is the abstract structure underlying problem-solving search: nodes represent states or situations, and edges are not stored explicitly but are generated on demand by a successor function that produces the states reachable from a given state (e.g. legal moves from a board position). Every search problem is defined by this implicit graph together with a designated start node (the initial state) and a goal test, a predicate that determines whether a given node counts as a solution. Because the graph is implicit, a search algorithm must generate nodes as it goes rather than reading them from a fixed data structure, and the same underlying state space can be searched in many different orders depending on the strategy used to decide which node to expand next.\n\n18. The branching factor b is the number of direct successors (children) a typical node has, i.e. how many actions or moves are available from a given state. The depth d refers to the depth of the shallowest goal node in the search tree, i.e. the length of the shortest path from the start node to a solution. These two quantities are the standard way of characterizing the size and difficulty of a search problem, and nearly all complexity analysis of search algorithms is expressed as a function of b and d (or b and m, the maximum depth, for algorithms without a known solution depth).\n\n19. Because each node at a given depth spawns roughly b new nodes at the next depth, the number of nodes at depth d grows approximately as b^d — exponential in the depth. This combinatorial explosion means that even modest branching factors and depths can produce astronomically large search spaces: doubling the depth does not double the work, it roughly squares it. Understanding this formula is essential for predicting when a naive search (like brute-force exhaustive search) will become computationally infeasible, and it motivates the need for smarter search strategies, heuristics, and pruning techniques.\n\n20. Chess is used as a concrete illustration of exponential blowup: with an average branching factor of about b≈35 (roughly 35 legal moves available in a typical position) and a search depth of around 50 ply (moves) needed to reach a decisive endgame, the number of nodes in the resulting search tree (about 35^50) vastly exceeds the estimated number of atoms in the observable universe (~10^80). This example makes vivid why exhaustive search is not a viable strategy for non-trivial problems and why real chess engines rely on heuristics, pruning (e.g. alpha-beta pruning), and limited-depth evaluation rather than exploring the full game tree.\n\n21. A good search strategy is ideally evaluated on several criteria: completeness (does it always find a goal node if one exists, even in infinite or very large spaces?), optimality (does it find the best goal, e.g. the shortest or lowest-cost path, rather than just any goal?), time complexity (how many nodes must be generated or expanded, typically expressed as a function of b and d), and space complexity (how much memory is needed to store the frontier and/or explored nodes, also typically a function of b and d). These four criteria are used throughout the study of search algorithms to compare strategies like breadth-first, depth-first, uniform-cost, and informed (heuristic) search, since no single strategy is uniformly best — each makes different trade-offs among completeness, optimality, time, and space.\n\n22. Depth-first search (DFS) explores by always expanding the most recently generated node, implemented using a stack (either explicitly or via recursion). This means it plunges as deep as possible along one branch before backtracking to try alternatives. Its major advantage is memory efficiency: it only needs to store the nodes along the current path plus their unexplored siblings, giving a space complexity of O(b·d), where b is the branching factor and d is the depth of the path. However, DFS is neither complete nor optimal in general graphs: if the search space contains infinite paths or cycles (without cycle checking), DFS can descend forever down one branch and never find a goal even if one exists elsewhere, and even when it does find a goal, that goal is not guaranteed to be reached via the shortest or cheapest path, since DFS commits to depth over breadth.\n\n23. Breadth-first search (BFS) explores the search space level by level, expanding all nodes at a given depth before moving to the next depth, using a queue (first-in, first-out) to manage the frontier of generated-but-unexpanded nodes. Because it exhausts each shallower layer before going deeper, BFS is guaranteed to find a solution if one exists (completeness), and when all edge costs are equal (uniform-cost edges), the first goal it finds is guaranteed to be reachable via the fewest edges, making it optimal under that condition. The price for these guarantees is space: BFS must keep an entire frontier of nodes in memory simultaneously, which grows exponentially with depth as O(b^d), where b is the branching factor and d is the solution depth — this exponential memory requirement is what makes BFS impractical for large or deep search spaces despite its correctness guarantees.\n\n24. DFS and BFS are both classified as 'uninformed' (or 'blind') search strategies because they operate using only the raw structure of the search graph — which nodes exist and how they connect — with no additional knowledge about which nodes are more promising or closer to the goal. Neither algorithm can tell, when choosing which node to expand next, whether that choice is moving the search closer to or farther from the goal; they simply follow their fixed rule (last-in stack order for DFS, first-in queue order for BFS) regardless of how far any given node actually is from a solution. This blindness is precisely what distinguishes them from informed (heuristic-based) search strategies, and it is the central limitation that motivates introducing heuristics and informed search methods, which use estimates of distance-to-goal to guide expansion more efficiently.\n\n25. The impracticality of uninformed search on large problems can be made concrete with real branching-factor and depth numbers. In chess, the branching factor is roughly 35 legal moves per position, so exploring even a modest number of plies ahead produces an astronomically large search tree, quickly exceeding any feasible memory or time budget for BFS and making even DFS impractical to run to completion. In real-world route planning over a road network (e.g., Germany), while the branching factor per intersection is small, the depth (number of steps) needed to reach distant destinations, combined with the sheer size of the graph, means naive uninformed search explores far more of the map than necessary, motivating more targeted methods. In LLM decoding, the 'branching factor' at each generation step is the vocabulary size (on the order of ~50,000 possible tokens), and sequences run 100 to 1000 tokens long — exhaustively searching this space with BFS or DFS is utterly infeasible (b^d is inconceivably large), which is why practical decoding uses greedy, sampling, or beam search rather than exhaustive uninformed search.\n\n26. Taken together, these examples establish the core trade-off between DFS and BFS and set up the motivation for informed search. DFS is cheap in space (O(b·d)) but risks incompleteness and non-optimal solutions; BFS guarantees completeness and optimality (for uniform costs) but is expensive in space (O(b^d)). Both, being uninformed, waste effort exploring directions that domain knowledge could immediately rule out as unpromising. The chess, road-network, and LLM examples show that realistic branching factors and depths push both algorithms' costs beyond feasibility, which is the practical argument for moving to informed search strategies (such as greedy best-first search or A*) that use a heuristic function to estimate distance to the goal and prioritize expansion accordingly, avoiding the wasted blind exploration inherent to DFS and BFS.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 27 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array."
