lly infeasible even though TicTacToe (9!≈363k sequences, 3^9≈19.7k distinct positions) is just barely tractable. The fix is depth-limited MinMax: search only to a bounded depth k, and evaluate the resulting non-terminal nodes with a heuristic evaluation function (e.g. material/positional advantage in chess, material/mill-count/mobility in Nine Men's Morris) instead of a true game-theoretic value. This trades exactness for tractability, with runtime O(c^m) for depth bound m assuming constant-time heuristics.\n\n3. In a chance-free game, only the relative ordering of the evaluation function's values across positions determines the optimal move — not the exact numeric values. For example, evaluations Win=1/Draw=0/Loss=-1 and Win=100/Draw=50/Loss=10 both rank Win > Draw > Loss and thus yield the same optimal move, whereas Win=1/Draw=0/Loss=0 (Win > Draw = Loss) has a different ordering and can lead to a different optimal move. This property justifies treating evaluation function design as primarily a matter of getting the ranking of situations right, and it later contrasts with games involving chance (expectiminimax), where exact values do matter.\n\n4. The Alpha-Beta algorithm augments MinMax with two bounds: α (the best value the Maximizer can currently guarantee) and β (the best value the Minimizer can currently guarantee), initially called as AlphaBeta(state, player, -∞, +∞). While iterating over a Minimizer node's successors, it tracks the running minimum βl and stops early (cutoff) once βl ≤ α, since the Maximizer would never let play reach this branch; the symmetric rule applies for Maximizer nodes using α and β. This yields exactly the same move choice as plain MinMax, just faster.\n\n5. Alpha-Beta's worst-case runtime is the same as plain MinMax, O(c^d), but with good move ordering — examining the strongest moves first — it drops to about O(c^(d/2)), roughly halving the effective branching factor and letting the search go twice as deep for the same computationa