ate the true expected value of winning as closely as possible, not just get comparisons right.\n\n9. MCTS departs from classical search in three ways: it samples the search space via random simulations instead of exhaustively exploring it, it uses simulation (playouts) rather than a static evaluation function to judge non-terminal states, and it expands the tree asymmetrically, investing more effort in promising branches rather than treating all branches equally. This makes it especially effective for games with a very high branching factor, such as Go (~250 legal moves per position) versus chess (~35), where classical Alpha-Beta search becomes impractical.\n\n10. Each MCTS iteration runs four phases: (1) Selection — traverse the tree from the root, choosing child nodes according to a selection policy, until reaching an expandable leaf; (2) Expansion — add one or more new child nodes to the tree at that leaf; (3) Simulation — run a random playout (rollout) from the new node to a terminal game state; (4) Backpropagation — propagate the simulation's result back up the path to the root, updating each visited node's win/visit statistics. Repeating this loop many times builds up statistics that let the algorithm converge on strong moves without exhaustive search.\n\n11. MCTS's selection phase typically uses UCT (Upper Confidence bound for Trees): UCT(v) = w_v/n_v + c·√(ln N_v / n_v), where w_v is the number of wins through node v, n_v is the number of simulations through v, N_v is the number of simulations through v's parent, and c is a tuning constant. The first term rewards exploitation of nodes with a good win rate so far; the second term rewards exploration of less-visited nodes (its value shrinks as n_v grows, and grows as sibling visits N_v grow). Balancing these two terms is what allows MCTS to focus search on promising moves while still occasionally investigating under-explored ones.\n\n12. AlphaGo (DeepMind) defeated Lee Sedol 4:1 in 2016 and AlphaGo Master defeated world #1 Ke Jie in 2017 — the first computer program to beat a top human Go player without a handicap. Go was long considered especially hard for AI due to its huge state space (~10^170 positions vs. ~10^50 for chess), high branching factor (~250 vs. ~35 for chess), and the difficulty of evaluating positions even for human experts — making classical Alpha-Beta/MinMax impractical. AlphaGo's breakthrough was combining MCTS with deep neural networks.\n\n13. AlphaGo combines MCTS with two deep neural networks: a policy network, trained via supervised learning on expert human games (and further refined by reinforcement learning), which proposes promising moves and replaces the purely random move selection of traditional MCTS; and a value network, which estimates the win probability of a position directly, reducing reliance on full random playout simulations for evaluation. MCTS uses both networks together — the policy network guides tree expansion/move selection, and the value network (combined with rollout evaluation) scores leaf nodes — to select the best move.\n\n14. Beyond classical board games, MCTS is applied to decision-making problems (autonomous driving path planning, robotics, multi-agent systems) and optimization problems (combinatorial optimization, Bayesian optimization, hyperparameter search for machine learning models). MCTS is especially valuable when the problem space is too large for complete exploration but simulations/evaluations can be run efficiently. The lecture also draws a conceptual link between MCTS's exploration/exploitation balance and sampling strategies used in LLM text decoding, where there is a similar trade-off between diversity and quality of generated tokens.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 15 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array."
