2026-09-13 18:13:44,232 INFO fatass: free(): cwd=C:\Users\Qi\Documents\GitHub\fatass\home\courses\prototype\lectures add_dirs=[] silent=True permission_mode=bypassPermissions model=sonnet effort=None tools=Read,Write prompt="Below are numbered overview texts for individual learning 'points' from one lecture's notes. For EACH numbered overview, write a short, specific title (roughly 3-8 words) that names what that point is actually about — a proper name/label, NOT a restatement or truncation of the opening sentence. For example, for an overview beginning 'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule: the main lecture meets every Thursday...', a good title is 'Weekly Lecture and Exercise Schedule', not 'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule'.\n\n0. An evolutionary algorithm's core data structures are: an individual, usually encoded as a bitstring (chromosome), with sub-sequences called genes; a fitness function scoring how good an individual is; and a population, a (multi-)set of individuals. The algorithm iteratively produces a new population from the current one — analogous to breeding animals for a trait, e.g., repeatedly selecting and pairing pigs with the largest ears, hoping the trait spreads through the population.\n\n1. Biological evolution has no explicit optimization goal. Recombination drives diversification of individuals and spreads genes through the population, which helps resilience to infection or fluctuating environments (hunger, heat, cold). Importantly, optimizing one trait can worsen another — e.g., pigs bred for huge ears may end up limping — a reminder that fitness functions in artificial EAs also involve trade-offs.\n\n2. The general genetic-search algorithm: start from an initial population S; while no individual reaches maximal fitness, build a new population S' by repeatedly (K times) selecting two fitness-weighted parents A and B from S, recombining them into offspring C, mutating C with low probability into C', and inserting C' into S'; then replace S with S'. The output is the fittest individual found (in the final generation). Selection uses randomness weighted by fitness so higher-fitness individuals are more likely to reproduce.\n\n3. Selection chooses which individuals from generation n contribute to generation n+1, with higher fitness giving a higher probability of being chosen. Selection probability can be assigned proportionally to raw fitness (sensitive to exact fitness values and very flat near optima/plateaus), proportional to an individual's rank within the population (e.g. best is 2–3x more likely than worst, tolerating a less precise fitness function), or proportional to a normalized fitness (e.g., fitness minus the minimum fitness in the population).\n\n4. Recombination (crossover) selects two parents (via selection), picks a random cut point in the bitstring, and splices the two parents together at that point to build an offspring. Mutation randomly flips individual bits of a chromosome (0→1 or 1→0), typically with a low probability, to introduce novel variation the population may not already contain. Extinction removes individuals (the worst, or a random one) when the population grows too large; evolution stops once an optimal individual is likely found.\n\n5. A genetic algorithm operates purely on bitstrings, where any bitstring is a valid result of the operators. An evolutionary algorithm generalizes this: it may use other sequence types (e.g., sequences of integers), restrict individuals to only valid/meaningful sequences, and use operators specifically adapted to only ever produce valid offspring (e.g., operators that only yield permutations). Practical parameters to tune include mutation and crossover probabilities, how offspring count depends on fitness, and population size.\n\n6. Queens can be encoded as a sequence of n numbers in {1,...,n}, where the i-th number gives the row of the queen placed in column i, e.g. [3,2,4,2,7,6,8,5]. This encoding allows invalid configurations, since a repeated value (here, 2 appears twice) means two queens share a column. Fitness is defined as the number of non-attacking queen pairs: worst possible value is 0 (every pair threatens each other), and the best possible value is C(n,2) = n(n-1)/2, achieved by a valid, non-attacking solution.\n\n7. With the free encoding, mutation randomly changes one value in the sequence to another value in {1,...,n} (moving one queen within its column/row), and recombination cuts two parent sequences at a point and splices them, which can also generate invalid (duplicate-containing) offspring. A better encoding restricts individuals to permutations of [1,...,n], guaranteeing exactly one queen per row/column; but then recombination almost always yields an invalid permutation, requiring fixes such as immediate extinction of invalid offspring, random repair, or simply skipping recombination — leaving mutation (swapping two elements) as the main operator, which behaves similarly to hill-climbing/best-first search.\n\n8. Consider a fixed gene G (a bit pattern at a fixed position). Let V be the whole population and S the individuals carrying G, with fitness a for members of S and lower fitness b for members of V\\S. If the next generation is formed by drawing |V| individuals from V with replacement, weighted by fitness, the probability of drawing an individual from S is p(S) = a|S| / (a|S| + b(|V|-|S|)), and from V\\S is the complementary expression. The expected size of the new S is p(S)·|V|, and S grows in relative terms whenever a > b.\n\n9. Defining rel(S(t)) as the relative share of S in generation t gives the recursion rel(S(t+1)) = a·rel(S(t)) / (a·rel(S(t)) + b·(1-rel(S(t)))). For the concrete case a = 2b (the gene is twice as fit) this simplifies to rel(t+1) = 2·rel(t)/(rel(t)+1); starting at rel(S(1)) = 10%, the share climbs 10% → 18% → 31% → 47% → 64% → 78% → 88% → 93% → 97% → 98% → 99%, permanently exceeding 90% by about generation 7. This numerically demonstrates how quickly a fitness advantage propagates through a population under pure selection.\n\n10. In the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP), n cities with connections must be toured via the shortest route visiting every city exactly once. An evolutionary approach encodes an individual as a permutation of all cities (the tour order), with fitness as the negative tour length (to be maximized). Mutation must preserve the permutation property, e.g. by swapping two cities' positions, and recombination needs a repair mechanism since naive crossover of two permutations usually yields an invalid tour; one proposed operator is partial inversion — inverting a sub-tour.\n\n11. The transportation problem (after Michalewicz, 1992) ships goods from warehouses with fixed stock to destinations with fixed demand at given per-unit costs, minimizing total cost (a worked instance with 3 warehouses and 4 destinations has optimal cost 315). Two encodings are possible: (1) the solution matrix itself as the individual — exact, but many individuals (including most produced by mutation/crossover) are invalid; or (2) a matrix of priorities (a permutation of 1..12) — always valid by construction, but crossover breaks the permutation and needs repair. Operators include moving a unit of goods between routes (encoding 1), or swapping/inverting priorities (encoding 2).\n\n12. NASA's Space Technology 5 (ST5) mission used an evolutionary algorithm to design the wire shape of an X-band antenna meeting difficult requirements (wide beamwidth for circular polarization plus wide bandwidth). The individual encoded antenna geometry, and fitness was based on radiation performance. The evolved design was a bizarre, 'bent' shape no human engineer would have proposed, yet it outperformed a traditionally hand-designed antenna — becoming the first computer-evolved hardware flown in space (launched March 2006).\n\n13. Neural Architecture Search (NAS) uses evolutionary search to design neural network architectures instead of hand-crafting them: the individual is a network architecture (layers and connections), fitness is validation accuracy after brief training, and mutation adds/removes layers or swaps operations. AmoebaNet (Real et al., 2019) is a landmark example — an evolutionarily-discovered architecture that was, at the time, state of the art for image classification, directly connecting to the machine-learning material covered elsewhere in the course.\n\n14. Evolution Strategies (OpenAI, 2017) offer an alternative to gradient-based training of reinforcement learning agents: the individual is a policy's parameters (weights), and fitness is the reward achieved in the environment, requiring no backpropagation — just 'try and score.' Compared to gradient methods, which need a differentiable objective and are often faster per step but sensitive to noisy rewards, evolutionary methods need only an evaluation, are extremely parallelizable, and are robust to noise — leading to the rule of thumb: use gradients when available, evolution when not.\n\n15. FunSearch and AlphaEvolve (Google DeepMind, 2023/2025) combine a large language model with evolutionary search to discover algorithms: the individual is a small program, mutation is the LLM proposing code changes (replacing random bit flips), fitness is an automated test scoring the program, and selection keeps the best programs for further evolution. This process has produced new mathematical results and faster algorithms, with evolution supplying the search process and the LLM supplying 'intelligent' mutations — turning a 30-year-old technique into a cutting-edge AI-driven discovery tool.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 16 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array."
