2026-09-13 18:13:54,007 INFO fatass: free(): cwd=C:\Users\Qi\Documents\GitHub\fatass\home\courses\prototype\lectures add_dirs=[] silent=True permission_mode=bypassPermissions model=sonnet effort=None tools=Read,Write prompt="Below are numbered overview texts for individual learning 'points' from one lecture's notes. For EACH numbered overview, write a short, specific title (roughly 3-8 words) that names what that point is actually about — a proper name/label, NOT a restatement or truncation of the opening sentence. For example, for an overview beginning 'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule: the main lecture meets every Thursday...', a good title is 'Weekly Lecture and Exercise Schedule', not 'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule'.\n\n0. Machine learning (ML) is a subfield of artificial intelligence, with deep learning in turn a subfield of ML. Tom Mitchell's classic definition frames ML as a computer program that learns from experience E with respect to some class of tasks T and performance measure P, improving its performance at T as measured by P through experience. The key distinguishing idea is that ML systems build predictive models directly from data rather than being explicitly programmed with hand-crafted rules: instead of a human specifying the logic step by step, the algorithm infers patterns and relationships from examples, producing a model that can generalize to new, unseen data.\n\n1. ML approaches are commonly divided into three paradigms. Supervised learning trains a model on labeled examples (input-output pairs) to predict outputs for new inputs, covering tasks like classification and regression. Unsupervised learning works with unlabeled data to discover hidden structure, such as clustering similar items or reducing dimensionality. Reinforcement learning trains an agent to make sequential decisions by interacting with an environment, receiving rewards or penalties, and learning a policy that maximizes cumulative reward over time. Understanding which paradigm applies is essential because it determines what kind of data is needed and what kind of model and evaluation are appropriate.\n\n2. Modern ML has enabled a range of prominent real-world applications that illustrate the different paradigms in action: large language models (trained largely via self-supervised and supervised techniques on massive text corpora) power conversational AI and text generation; AlphaFold uses deep learning to predict protein 3D structures from amino acid sequences, transforming structural biology; medical diagnosis systems classify patient data (images, lab results) to assist clinicians in detecting disease; and recommender systems use patterns in user behavior to predict preferences and suggest relevant items, as seen on streaming and e-commerce platforms.\n\n3. Classification is a supervised learning task where the goal is to predict a discrete class label for an object based on its attributes (features). For example, given measurements of an email (word frequencies, sender information), a classifier predicts whether it is 'spam' or 'not spam'. To build and evaluate such a model, data is typically split into a training set (used to fit the model) and a test set (held out to assess how well the model generalizes to new data). Stratification ensures that this split preserves the original proportion of each class in both subsets, which is especially important for imbalanced datasets. Cross-validation extends this idea by repeatedly partitioning the data into multiple train/test folds, training and evaluating the model on each fold, and averaging the results to obtain a more robust estimate of model performance and reduce the risk of a lucky or unlucky single split.\n\n4. When evaluating a binary classifier, predictions are categorized using a confusion matrix into four outcomes: true positives (TP, correctly predicted positive cases), true negatives (TN, correctly predicted negative cases), false positives (FP, negative cases incorrectly predicted as positive, i.e., a 'false alarm'), and false negatives (FN, positive cases incorrectly predicted as negative, i.e., a 'miss'). The pregnancy-test example illustrates this clearly: a true positive is a pregnant person correctly testing positive, a false positive is a non-pregnant person incorrectly testing positive, a true negative is a non-pregnant person correctly testing negative, and a false negative is a pregnant person incorrectly testing negative. These four counts form the foundation for computing further metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and specificity.\n\n5. The confusion matrix (true positives, false positives, true negatives, false negatives) underlies a family of evaluation metrics, each answering a different question about classifier performance. Recall (sensitivity) is TP / (TP + FN): of all actual positive cases, what fraction did the classifier catch? Specificity is TN / (TN + FP): of all actual negative cases, what fraction did the classifier correctly reject? On the pregnancy-test example, recall measures how many truly pregnant women test positive, while specificity measures how many truly non-pregnant women test negative. These two metrics focus on performance conditioned on the true label, and a classifier can score very differently on each — a test that always predicts 'positive' has perfect recall but zero specificity.\n\n6. Precision is TP / (TP + FP): of all cases the classifier predicted positive, what fraction were actually positive? Negative predictive value (NPV) is TN / (TN + FN): of all cases predicted negative, what fraction were actually negative? Unlike recall and specificity, which condition on the true label, precision and NPV condition on the predicted label, capturing how trustworthy a positive or negative prediction actually is. On the pregnancy-test example, precision asks: among women who tested positive, how many are truly pregnant? Precision is especially sensitive to class imbalance and the base rate of the positive class.\n\n7. The F-measure (F1 score) is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall). The harmonic mean is used instead of the arithmetic mean because it penalizes extreme imbalance between the two components — a classifier can only score well on F1 if both precision and recall are reasonably high, not just one of them. This makes F1 a useful single-number summary when both false positives and false negatives matter and no single metric like accuracy alone captures the tradeoff.\n\n8. The rare-disease ('yellow fever' fever-based classifier) example illustrates a key pitfall: when the positive class is rare, a classifier that flags almost everyone with a fever as having the disease can achieve very high recall (catching nearly all true cases) while having very poor precision (most flagged cases are false alarms, since fever is common but the disease is rare). This drags the F-score toward zero despite the seemingly impressive recall, demonstrating why no single metric should be trusted in isolation and why metrics must be weighed jointly against the problem's class balance and costs of different error types.\n\n9. Many classifiers output a continuous score or probability rather than a hard label, requiring a threshold to convert scores into positive/negative predictions; changing that threshold trades recall against precision/specificity. The ROC curve plots the true positive rate (recall) against the false positive rate (1 - specificity) across all possible thresholds, and the area under the ROC curve (AUC) summarizes overall ranking quality independent of any single threshold choice. The precision-recall curve instead plots precision against recall across thresholds, which is often more informative than ROC curves when the positive class is rare, since ROC/AUC can look deceptively good under class imbalance.\n\n10. There is no universally 'best' evaluation metric: the right choice depends on the specific domain, the problem's tolerance for false positives versus false negatives, the class balance in the dataset, and the algorithm being evaluated. For example, a medical screening test may prioritize recall to avoid missing true cases, while a spam filter may prioritize precision to avoid blocking legitimate messages, and these tradeoffs must be made deliberately rather than defaulting to accuracy or a single generic score.\n\n11. Classifier families: k-nearest neighbors, support vector machines, decision trees, and random forests can all be used to assign a class label to an input, but they differ in whether the decision boundary they learn is linear or non-linear. A linear classifier (like a basic SVM with a linear kernel) separates classes with a straight line, plane, or hyperplane, which works well when classes are linearly separable but fails on more complex data. Non-linear classifiers (k-NN, decision trees, random forests, kernelized SVMs) can carve out curved or piecewise decision regions, letting them fit more complex data at the cost of being more prone to overfitting and often being less interpretable. Understanding this taxonomy is the foundation for choosing an appropriate model and for appreciating why decision trees, the focus of the rest of the lecture, take a distinctly non-linear, rule-based approach to classification.\n\n12. Information content and entropy: for an event a with probability p(a), its information content (or 'surprisal') is defined as I(a) = -log2(p(a)), measured in bits. Rare events carry high information content (learning they happened is very informative) while near-certain events carry little. Entropy H is the expected value of information content over a whole probability distribution, H = -sum(p(a) * log2(p(a))), and it measures the average uncertainty or 'impurity' of a distribution. A uniform distribution over n outcomes maximizes entropy (log2(n) bits), while a highly skewed distribution (one outcome near-certain) has entropy near zero. The Bernoulli/coin-flip case illustrates this well: entropy as a function of p forms a symmetric curve peaking at 1 bit when p=0.5 (maximum uncertainty) and dropping to 0 bits as p approaches 0 or 1 (no uncertainty), and this curve is the standard example used to build intuition for entropy before applying it to trees.\n\n13. Building blocks of decision trees: a decision tree classifies examples by asking a sequence of questions about attributes (features), branching at each internal node based on the attribute's value, until reaching a leaf node that predicts a concept (class label). Trees can be simple (few nodes, shallow, easy to interpret, but possibly underfitting) or complex (many nodes, deep, capturing fine-grained patterns, but risking overfitting). The classic 'good apple' example uses attributes like taste, color, and circumference to predict whether an apple is 'good'; each attribute becomes a candidate splitting point, and the tree-building problem is fundamentally about choosing, at each step, which attribute best separates the examples into purer (lower-entropy) subgroups.\n\n14. The ID3 algorithm and information gain: ID3 builds a decision tree recursively and greedily by, at each node, selecting the attribute that maximizes information gain — the reduction in entropy achieved by splitting the current set of examples on that attribute. Formally, information gain for attribute A is Gain(S, A) = H(S) - sum over values v of A of (|S_v|/|S|) * H(S_v), where S is the current example set and S_v is the subset with attribute value v. The algorithm picks the attribute with the highest gain, creates a branch for each of its values, and recurses on each resulting subset until nodes are pure (single class) or no attributes remain. In the 'good apple' example, this means comparing the information gain from splitting on taste, color, or circumference first, and building the tree top-down accordingly.\n\n15. ID3's bias toward many-valued attributes and the C4.5 fix: information gain systematically favors attributes with many distinct values (e.g., an ID-like attribute with a unique value per example), because splitting on such an attribute can drive subset entropy to zero even though the split is not meaningfully predictive and generalizes poorly. C4.5 corrects this by using the gain ratio instead of raw information gain: it normalizes information gain by the 'split information' (the entropy of the attribute's own value distribution), penalizing attributes that split the data into many small, fragmented groups. This normalized measure favors attributes that are genuinely more discriminative rather than merely more fragmenting.\n\n16. Overfitting, overgeneralization, and pruning: decision trees grown to full depth tend to overfit — they capture noise and idiosyncrasies of the training data (overfitting) or, conversely, may miss meaningful structure if grown too shallow or with overly coarse splits (overgeneralization). Overfit trees perform well on training data but poorly on unseen data because they encode spurious patterns. Pruning addresses this by simplifying the tree after (post-pruning) or during (pre-pruning) construction — removing branches that do not improve validation performance, or that fail statistical significance tests — trading a small increase in training error for better generalization and a more interpretable, robust model.\n\n17. The bias-variance decomposition formalizes a model's expected prediction error as the sum of three terms: bias squared, variance, and irreducible noise. Bias measures how far the average prediction (over different training sets) is from the true function, capturing systematic error from a model being too simple to represent the underlying relationship. Variance measures how much predictions fluctuate across different training sets, capturing sensitivity to the specific data sampled. Irreducible error is noise inherent to the data-generating process that no model can eliminate. This decomposition explains the classic U-shaped test-error curve: as model complexity increases, bias tends to fall while variance rises, and the goal of model selection is to find the sweet spot that minimizes their sum.\n\n18. Single decision trees, especially when grown deep, exhibit low bias but high variance. Because a deep tree can partition the feature space into arbitrarily small regions, it can fit the training data (including its noise) almost perfectly, giving it very low bias. However, this same flexibility makes trees unstable: a small change in the training data (removing or adding a few points) can lead to a very different sequence of splits near the root, cascading into a substantially different tree structure and predictions. This instability is precisely high variance, and it is the reason trees are considered strong candidates for variance-reduction techniques rather than bias-reduction ones.\n\n19. Bagging (bootstrap aggregating) reduces variance by training many copies of a model on different bootstrap samples (random samples drawn with replacement from the training set) and averaging their predictions (or taking a majority vote for classification). Because each bootstrap sample differs slightly, each model makes somewhat different errors, and averaging cancels out much of that noise while leaving the shared, systematic signal (and bias) largely intact. Bagging is especially effective for high-variance, low-bias base learners like deep decision trees, since it directly targets the component of error where trees struggle most, without requiring any change to how each individual tree is built.\n\n20. Random forests extend bagging specifically for decision trees by adding a second source of randomness: at each split in each tree, only a random subset of features is considered as candidates, rather than all available features. This feature subsampling decorrelates the trees in the ensemble — without it, trees built on bootstrap samples of the same data tend to make similar early splits on the strongest predictors, so averaging them reduces variance less than it could. By forcing trees to sometimes ignore the dominant feature, random forests produce a more diverse set of trees whose errors are less correlated, which improves the variance-reduction benefit of averaging beyond what plain bagging achieves.\n\n21. Boosting is a complementary strategy that reduces bias by combining many weak learners (models only slightly better than random guessing) sequentially, where each new learner focuses on correcting the mistakes of the ensemble built so far. AdaBoost does this by reweighting training examples after each round: points misclassified by the current ensemble receive increased weight, forcing the next weak learner to focus more on the hard cases, and the final prediction is a weighted vote of all the weak learners. Gradient boosting generalizes this idea by fitting each new model to the residual errors (or more precisely, the negative gradient of a loss function) of the current ensemble, allowing it to optimize arbitrary differentiable loss functions; this approach underlies popular implementations like XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost.\n\n22. Despite the rise of deep learning, tree-based ensemble methods — random forests and gradient-boosted trees in particular — remain highly competitive and often state-of-the-art on structured, tabular data. They tend to require less feature engineering and hyperparameter tuning than neural networks for such data, handle mixed feature types and missing values gracefully, and are computationally efficient to train, which explains their continued dominance in industry applications and machine learning competitions involving tabular datasets.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 23 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-resu