values changes the resulting optimal policy.\n\n2. The Markov property states that the probability of the next state depends only on the current state and action, not on the full history of prior states and actions. This means the current state must encapsulate all information relevant to predicting the future; in Gridworld the agent's position alone suffices, but a system with inertia (where future motion depends on past velocity, not just position) would violate the Markov property unless velocity is added to the state representation.\n\n3. Because the cumulative reward U(s0) = Σ R(sk,ak) may not converge over an infinite horizon, a discount factor γ ∈ [0,1] geometrically down-weights future rewards: U(s0) = Σ γ^k R(sk,ak). This guarantees convergence for bounded rewards and models the intuition that immediate rewards matter more than distant ones. As γ→0 the agent becomes myopic (only immediate reward matters); as γ→1 it becomes far-sighted (future rewards weighted almost as heavily as immediate ones). Since transitions are stochastic, the true optimization objective is the *expected* cumulative discounted reward, E[U(s0)], which the agent seeks to maximize via its policy.\n\n4. A policy π: S → A assigns an action to every state, in contrast to a fixed action sequence which cannot adapt to different stochastic outcomes. The optimal policy π* maximizes the expected cumulative discounted reward from every state: π* ∈ arg max_π E_π[U(s)]. For any finite MDP, there always exists a deterministic optimal policy — so randomizing actions is never strictly necessary to achieve optimality.\n\n5. The Bellman equation recursively defines the optimal value U(s) of a state as the best achievable combination of the immediate reward and the discounted expected value of the successor state, maximized over actions: U(s) = max_a [R(s,a) + γ Σ_s' P(s'|s,a) U(s')]. This turns the search for an optimal policy into a system of n nonlinear equations (one per state) that cannot be solved directly in closed form because of the max operator, motivating the iterative algorithms covered in the next topic.\n\n6. Value Iteration initializes U0(s)=0 for all states and repeatedly applies the Bellman update U_{k+1}(s) ← max_a [R(s,a) + γ Σ_s' P(s'|s,a) U_k(s')] until the value function stops changing (converges). In the worked Gridworld example (γ=0.9, step cost −0.04, goal +1, bad state −1), utility values propagate outward from the goal and converge after about 16 iterations; the final optimal policy is then extracted by taking the arg max action at each state using the converged utilities.\n\n7. Value Iteration converges because the Bellman update can be viewed as an operator B applied to the vector of state values, and B is a contraction mapping in the max-norm: ||B U − B U'||∞ ≤ γ ||U − U'||∞. Because γ < 1, repeated application of B shrinks the distance between any two value estimates by a factor of γ each iteration, so by the Banach fixed-point theorem the sequence converges to the unique fixed point of B, which is exactly the solution of the Bellman equations (the true optimal value function U*).\n\n8. Policy Iteration exploits the observation that the optimal policy typically stabilizes before the value function has fully converged numerically. It starts from an arbitrary policy π0 and alternates two steps: (1) policy evaluation, solving the *linear* system Uπ(s) = R(s,π(s)) + γ Σ_s' P(s'|s,π(s)) Uπ(s') exactly for the fixed policy (no max operator, so it is a standard linear system rather than a nonlinear one); and (2) policy improvement, greedily updating the policy at every state using the just-computed value function: π'(s) = arg max_a [R(s,a) + γ Σ_s' P(s'|s,a) Uπ(s')]. The algorithm terminates when the policy stops changing, which is provably the optimal policy π*.\n\n9. Value Iteration and Policy Iteration both perform dynamic programming based on the Bellman principle of optimality (an optimal solution is built from optimal sub-solutions), but differ in what they iterate on and how. Value Iteration iterates on the value function using the Bellman max-update, usually needs many iterations, and only yields a usable policy at the very end. Policy Iteration iterates directly on policies, needs a linear-system solve plus a greedy improvement step per iteration, typically converges in far fewer iterations, and has a valid (though possibly suboptimal) policy available after every single step.\n\n10. A POMDP extends the MDP tuple (S,A,P,R,γ) with two additional components: Ω, the set of possible observations, and O(o|s), the observation model giving the probability of observing o when the true state is s. The core difficulty is that the agent never directly observes its true state s, only an observation o, and different states can produce the same observation — so the agent cannot simply apply the MDP machinery to the (unknown) true state.\n\n11. Instead of tracking an exact state, a POMDP agent maintains a belief state b(s), a probability distribution over possible true states. After taking action a and receiving observation o, the belief is updated via a Bayes filter: b'(s') = η · O(o|s') · Σ_s P(s'|s,a) b(s), where η is a normalizing constant. This update has two conceptual steps: prediction (propagate the old belief through the transition model P) and filtering/correction (reweight the predicted belief by how well each candidate state s' explains the new observation via O(o|s')).\n\n12. A POMDP can be reformulated as an MDP defined over belief states rather than raw states: the belief plays the role of the state, the Bayes update plays the role of the transition function, the expected reward Σ_s b(s)R(s,a) plays the role of the reward, and the policy maps beliefs to actions (π: b → a) instead of states to actions. This yields an analogous Bellman equation over beliefs: U(b) = max_a [Σ_s b(s)R(s,a) + γ Σ_o P(o|b,a) U(b'_{a,o})]. The key added difficulty compared to standard MDPs is that the belief space is continuous (an infinite state space), so exact solutions are rarely practical and approximation methods are required.\n\n13. When ML systems make wrong predictions, the consequences range from irrelevant (an ad recommendation) to critical (a treatment recommendation). In medicine and other physical-world applications, models must communicate their uncertainty so that users can distinguish reliable from unreliable predictions — separating 'known unknowns' (quantifiable uncertainty) from 'unknown unknowns.' A concrete failure mode: a skin-lesion classifier trained on one hospital's images can confidently (99% confidence) misclassify a melanoma as benign when applied to images from a different hospital/population due to data drift — demonstrating that standard networks are not calibrated or reliable and cannot distinguish their own correct from incorrect high-confidence predictions.\n\n14. Calibration formalizes the idea that predicted class scores should represent the true likelihood of the underlying event. Canonical calibration is the strongest and most general form: the full model output f(X) should equal the true conditional distribution of the label, f(X) = P(Y|f(X)). Top-label calibration is the weakest, most commonly used form: only the confidence of the predicted (arg max) class must match its true correctness probability, f_K(X) = P(Y=K|f_K(X)) where K=argmax_k f_k(X). There is a spectrum of calibration definitions between these extremes, and choosing which one to target is itself a design decision that affects downstream conclusions about trustworthiness.\n\n15. Because the true conditional distribution P(Y|f(X)) is unknown and only one label sample exists per prediction, calibration error must be estimated, typically via binning (Expected Calibration Error, ECE: split predictions into confidence bins, compare mean confidence vs. mean accuracy per bin, and take a weighted average of the gaps) or kernel density estimation (KDE). In practice, computing a calibration error requires three choices: the calibration definition (canonical vs. top-label), the distance function, and the estimator. Crucially, commonly used calibration estimators are biased and their bias scales with dataset size — shown empirically to affect CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet benchmarks — so calibration numbers must be interpreted with this bias in mind, especially when comparing across models or datasets of different sizes.\n\n16. Post-hoc calibration adjusts a trained model's output probabilities after training, without changing its weights or architecture, and typically without affecting accuracy. The most common technique is temperature scaling: divide the logits z by a single learned scalar temperature T before the softmax, Q̂ = max_c softmax(z/T)(c), with T fit on an independent validation set (T=1 is no change, T>1 softens/reduces overconfidence, T<1 sharpens confidence). Extensions include accounting for distribution shift via generic perturbations during calibration, and learning prediction-specific/adaptive temperatures rather than one global value. In practice, post-hoc calibrated models should always be re-validated by computing calibration error on an independent test set, since calibration quality is application-dependent and what works for natural-image benchmarks may not transfer to specialized domains like medical imaging.\n\n17. Uncertainty in a prediction reflects either randomness or a lack of knowledge about the target variable. Aleatoric uncertainty is irreducible randomness inherent to the data-generating process — it cannot be reduced even with unlimited data. Epistemic uncertainty stems from limited data or knowledge and can, in principle, be reduced by collecting more/better data; it should be high in low-data regions and for out-of-distribution inputs. Quantifying epistemic uncertainty remains an open research question: one principled approach is via a bias-variance decomposition of the model's loss; commonly used practical alternatives include distributional approaches (e.g., SWAG, Evidential Deep Learning), Deep Ensembles, MC Dropout, and other specialized deterministic methods.\n\n18. Two widely used practical methods for estimating epistemic uncertainty are MC Dropout and Deep Ensembles. MC Dropout keeps dropout active at inference time and performs multiple stochastic forward passes through a single trained network, using the variance across passes as an uncertainty estimate (theoretically justified as approximating a deep Gaussian process). Deep Ensembles instead train several independently initialized copies of the same architecture and use the disagreement/variance across their predictions as the uncertainty estimate. Deep Ensembles generally give more reliable, better-decorrelated uncertainty estimates than MC Dropout (which tends to underestimate uncertainty, especially out-of-distribution), but at higher computational cost since multiple full networks must be trained and stored.\n\n19. Bregman Information (BI) is a method for quantifying epistemic uncertainty about the data-generating process directly in logit space. It is computed via test-time augmentation (TTA): generate P augmented versions of an input, collect their logit vectors ẑ_i, and compute u(x)_BI = (1/P)Σ LSE(ẑ_i) − LSE((1/P)Σ ẑ_i), where LSE is the log-sum-exp function. Unlike plain softmax confidence, BI has been shown to be robust to distribution shifts — remaining a reliable epistemic-uncertainty signal even when a model's raw confidence becomes miscalibrated under data drift. Epistemic uncertainty estimates like BI are especially useful for active learning/testing, continual/lifelong learning, detecting distribution shift, interactive settings, and triaging cases for human review.\n\n20. Uncertainty quantification extends naturally to generative models and large language models (LLMs), where the goal is often to detect hallucinations. A basic approach: ask the model the same question multiple times, embed the answers in a vector space, and compute a generalized variance of the answers as an uncertainty signal (motivated by the bias-variance decomposition of the kernel scoring rule). A more refined 'spectral uncertainty' approach goes beyond surface-level hallucination detection by handling ambiguous questions: an auxiliary LLM generates several clarifications of the original (possibly ambiguous) question (outer samples), the target LLM generates several answers per clarification (inner samples), the answers are embedded, kernel matrices are built per-clarification and overall, and the von Neumann entropy (VNE) of these kernel matrices — e.g., SpecE(Clarification_i) = −Tr[K_i log K_i] — is used as the uncertainty measure, capturing uncertainty due to ambiguity as well as randomness in generation.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 21 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array."
