2026-09-13 18:14:13,903 INFO fatass: free(): cwd=C:\Users\Qi\Documents\GitHub\fatass\home\courses\prototype\lectures add_dirs=[] silent=True permission_mode=bypassPermissions model=sonnet effort=None tools=Read,Write prompt='Below are numbered overview texts for individual learning \'points\' from one lecture\'s notes. For EACH numbered overview, write a short, specific title (roughly 3-8 words) that names what that point is actually about — a proper name/label, NOT a restatement or truncation of the opening sentence. For example, for an overview beginning \'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule: the main lecture meets every Thursday...\', a good title is \'Weekly Lecture and Exercise Schedule\', not \'The course follows a fixed weekly schedule\'.\n\n0. Classical machine learning trains one model per narrowly defined task, requiring separate feature engineering and datasets for each. Deep learning shifted this by letting networks learn their own representations from raw data. Foundation models go further: a single, very large model is pretrained (usually self-supervised) on broad, diverse data, and this one model can then be adapted — via fine-tuning or prompting — to a wide variety of downstream tasks, rather than being built from scratch each time.\n\nThis shift is accompanied by two key phenomena: emergence, where qualitatively new capabilities appear as models are scaled up (capabilities not present, or not predictable, in smaller models), and homogenization, where the field converges on a small number of underlying architectures (predominantly Transformers) used across many different applications and modalities.\n\n1. Segment Anything (SAM) is a foundation model for computer vision (not language), developed by Meta AI. Earlier vision segmentation used two separate approaches: interactive, human-guided segmentation and automatic, object-specific segmentation. SAM unifies this into a single promptable model — it can be prompted with points, boxes, masks, or text — trained on the SA-1B dataset containing about 1 billion masks, allowing it to generalize (zero-shot) to new domains and tasks it wasn\'t explicitly trained for. Its successor, SAM 2 (2024), extends the same promptable-segmentation idea to video, enabling object tracking across frames.\n\nSAM is a good example of the foundation model paradigm applied outside of language: one broadly pretrained model, adaptable via prompting, replacing many narrow, task-specific vision models.\n\n2. A language model (LM) is formally a probability distribution p(x1, x2, ..., xL) over sequences of tokens, where a token can be a word, a word-piece (subword), or even a single character (including punctuation/symbols). The LM assigns a plausibility score to any candidate sequence: grammatically and semantically sensible sequences should receive higher probability than nonsensical ones.\n\nFor example, given the vocabulary {the, mouse, ate, cheese, ball}, a good LM assigns p("the mouse ate the cheese") = 0.02, much higher than p("the cheese ate the mouse") = 0.01 (grammatical but implausible: mice eat cheese, not vice versa) and far higher than p("mouse the the cheese ate") = 0.0001 (ungrammatical). This shows the LM combines syntactic knowledge (word order/grammar) with world knowledge (mice eat cheese).\n\n3. Since directly modeling the joint probability of a whole sequence is intractable, LMs factorize it using the chain rule of probability: p(x1:L) = p(x1) · p(x2|x1) · p(x3|x1,x2) · ... = ∏_{i=1}^{L} p(xi | x1:i-1). Each conditional distribution p(xi | x1:i-1) — the probability of the next token given everything before it — is computed by a neural network.\n\nThis is exactly what makes modern LLMs autoregressive: the model never computes the full joint probability in one shot, but builds it up token by token, each time conditioning on all previously generated (or given) tokens. During training this is done in parallel via teacher forcing (using ground-truth previous tokens); during inference/generation it must proceed sequentially, one token at a time.\n\n4. To generate text, you provide a prefix (the prompt) and then sample the remaining tokens one at a time: for i = 1, ..., L: xi ~ p(xi | x1:i-1)^(1/T). The temperature T rescales the model\'s probability distribution before sampling and controls the randomness of generation.\n\nAt T=0, generation is deterministic — always the single most probable token is chosen (argmax). At T=1, tokens are sampled exactly according to the model\'s own learned distribution. As T→∞, the distribution flattens toward uniform, giving maximal randomness. In practice, higher temperature produces more creative/diverse text but also increases the risk of incoherent or factually wrong output; lower temperature produces more focused, conservative, repetitive text. Related sampling controls used in practice include top-k (restrict to the k most likely tokens) and top-p/nucleus sampling (restrict to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds a threshold p).\n\n5. Before 2017, sequence models like RNNs processed tokens step by step, which was slow (no parallelism across time steps) and prone to "forgetting" information from far back in the sequence (vanishing gradients / limited effective memory). The 2017 paper "Attention Is All You Need" introduced the Transformer, which processes the entire sequence at once rather than step by step.\n\nThe Transformer consists of a stack of encoder blocks and a stack of decoder blocks. Each encoder block contains a self-attention sub-layer followed by a position-wise feed-forward network (with residual connections and normalization, though not emphasized in this lecture\'s slides). Attention lets every token directly form a weighted combination of every other token\'s information, regardless of distance, and this can be computed in parallel across the whole sequence — a major reason Transformers train efficiently on GPUs.\n\n6. Self-attention is the mechanism by which each token in a sequence gathers relevant context from every other token. Intuitively, for a word like "it" in a sentence, self-attention lets the model figure out which earlier word (e.g. "the animal") it refers to — resolving references and dependencies without any hand-coded grammar rules. This behavior is entirely learned from data during training, not programmed explicitly.\n\nThis is the key building block that replaces recurrence: instead of passing a hidden state sequentially, every token can attend to (look at, and weight) every other token\'s representation directly.\n\n7. Self-attention computes three vectors from each token embedding x via learned weight matrices: query q = xW_Q, key k = xW_K, and value v = xW_V. These play the role familiar from information retrieval: the query represents "what am I looking for", the key represents "what do I match against" (each token advertises itself via its key), and the value represents "what content is actually returned" if there\'s a match.\n\nEvery token produces its own q, k, and v; a token\'s query is compared against every other token\'s key to determine how much attention to pay to that token\'s value.\n\n8. For a given query qi, the raw attention score against another token\'s key kj is the dot product: score_ij = qi · kj. This score is then scaled by 1/√d_k (where d_k is the key vector dimension) before applying softmax — this scaling prevents the dot products from growing too large in magnitude, which would push softmax into regions with extremely small gradients and destabilize training.\n\nAfter scaling, softmax converts the scores into a set of positive weights summing to 1 across all tokens being attended to — these are the attention weights.\n\n9. The final self-attention output for a token, zi, is the weighted sum of all value vectors, weighted by the softmax attention weights: zi = Σ_j softmax_j · vj. Every token gets its own contextualized output vector zi that blends information from all other tokens according to how much attention it pays to each. Crucially, this computation happens simultaneously for every position in the sequence — it is highly parallelizable, unlike the sequential processing in RNNs.\n\nIn compact matrix form, stacking all token embeddings as rows of a matrix X and computing Q = XW_Q, K = XW_K, V = XW_V, the entire operation is: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^T / √d_k) · V — just a handful of matrix multiplications, which is why Transformers run efficiently on GPUs.\n\n10. Self-attention on its own is permutation-invariant — it would treat "the animal bit the man" the same as "the man bit the animal" if word order weren\'t encoded somehow, since attention just computes weighted sums regardless of position. To fix this, a positional vector ti is added to each token embedding before attention, injecting information about each token\'s position in the sequence.\n\nThe original Transformer used fixed sinusoidal positional encodings. Modern LLMs mostly use RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding), which rotates query/key vectors in a way that encodes relative position directly into the attention computation and generalizes better to sequence lengths not seen during training.\n\n11. Instead of computing attention once, Transformers use multi-head attention: several attention "heads" run in parallel, each with its own independently learned W_Q, W_K, W_V matrices. Because each head is initialized and trained separately, different heads tend to specialize in learning different kinds of relationships between tokens — e.g., one head might focus on syntactic structure, another on coreference (which pronoun refers to which noun), another on part-of-speech patterns.\n\nThe outputs of all heads are concatenated and passed through a linear projection to produce the final attention output. In practice, models typically use 8–16 heads per attention layer. Running one attention computation would force all these different relationship types to be averaged together into a single representation; multiple heads let the model represent several relationship types simultaneously.\n\n12. After the final decoder block produces an output vector, a linear layer maps this vector to a single logit value per vocabulary entry (one score for every possible next token). Softmax then converts these logits into a probability distribution over the vocabulary. The next token is chosen either via argmax (always the highest-probability token, deterministic) or via sampling (introducing randomness, as controlled by temperature).\n\nAfter a token is chosen, it\'s appended to the sequence and the whole process repeats to generate the following token — this is what makes generation autoregressive: each new token is generated conditioned on all tokens generated so far.\n\n13. Pretraining trains a very large neural network on massive amounts of unlabeled text scraped from the internet (Wikipedia, Reddit, books, code, etc.), with the sole training objective of predicting the next word/token given the preceding context. This requires billions of parameters and enormous compute budgets (GPU-years, millions of dollars). The result is a base model (e.g. GPT-3 in 2020) that has absorbed a huge amount of linguistic and world knowledge simply by learning to compress and predict text.\n\nHowever, a base model only continues text plausibly — it does not inherently follow instructions or behave like an assistant; given a question, it might just continue with more questions rather than answering. Two further training stages (instruction fine-tuning and RLHF) are needed to turn it into something like a chatbot.\n\n14. Instruction fine-tuning takes the pretrained base model and continues training it, in a supervised way, on many pairs of (instruction, good answer) — the model learns to imitate the desired response style for a given instruction. Example datasets include Alpaca (52k instruction-response pairs, generated via self-instruct) and Baize (dialogue data). A worked example: instruction "Explain photosynthesis in one sentence" paired with the answer "Plants convert light, water, and CO2 into sugar and oxygen."\n\nAfter this stage the model reliably follows instructions rather than just continuing text arbitrarily. However, this alone doesn\'t resolve which of several plausible-sounding answers is actually better — that\'s the problem RLHF addresses next.\n\n15. RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) is the third training stage. First, humans compare and rank several candidate model responses to the same prompt by quality. Second, this ranking data is used to train a separate reward model rθ that learns to predict a scalar "quality" score for any given response — high for responses humans preferred, low otherwise. Third, the language model itself is fine-tuned using reinforcement learning to maximize the score given by rθ.\n\nFraming this as an RL problem: the policy is the language model, the action is choosing the next token, and the reward comes from the reward model\'s score of the full generated response. A KL-divergence penalty term is added to keep the fine-tuned model from drifting too far from the original base/instruction-tuned model (preventing reward hacking and degenerate outputs). A popular modern alternative is DPO (Direct Preference Optimization), which optimizes directly on preference pairs without needing to train a separate reward model or run full RL.\n\n16. Fine-tuning all billions of parameters of a large model for every new task is computationally expensive and memory-intensive. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is based on the observation that the necessary weight update ΔW during adaptation tends to have low intrinsic rank — it can be well approximated by the product of two much smaller matrices, ΔW = A·B^T, where A and B have far fewer parameters than the full weight matrix W.\n\nDuring LoRA fine-tuning, the original pretrained weight matrix W is frozen (not updated); only the small A and B matrices are trained. This drastically reduces the number of trainable parameters and the memory needed for fine-tuning, while achieving performance close to full fine-tuning on many tasks. LoRA is also widely used beyond language models, e.g., to adapt text-to-image diffusion models to new styles.\n\n17. An early milestone in multimodal foundation models was Flamingo (DeepMind, 2022), which combined a frozen large language model with visual inputs via learnable adapters, accepting interleaved text and images. As of 2026, frontier models (e.g. GPT-5, Gemini 3, Claude Opus 4.x) have moved beyond bolting a vision encoder onto a language model: they are natively multimodal, meaning a single Transformer architecture is trained end-to-end to handle text, images, audio, and video together, without a separate dedicated image encoder module.\n\n18. Diffusion is the other major breakthrough behind modern generative image models. During training, noise is progressively added to real images (the forward process) until nothing but pure random noise remains; a neural network is then trained to reverse this process, gradually removing noise step by step to recover a clean image. Generation starts from pure noise and repeatedly applies the learned denoising network to arrive at a realistic image.\n\nFor text-to-image generation (e.g. DALL·E-3, Stable Diffusion 3, FLUX, Imagen 3, Midjourney), a Transformer-based text encoder first encodes the input prompt, and this text representation conditions the diffusion process so that the generated image matches the description.\n\n19. LLM agents extend a language model from single-turn question answering into multi-step planning and acting using external tools — searching the web, executing code, calling APIs, controlling a computer ("computer-use" agents), or writing and running code ("coding agents"). Games serve as convenient testbeds for agent research: Voyager (2023) had an LLM play and progress through Minecraft autonomously, and DeepMind\'s SIMA is a generalist game agent; earlier, purely RL-based approaches like VPT (2022) tackled Minecraft without an LLM component.\n\nConceptually, agents combine planning (deciding what sequence of actions/tool calls to take) with reinforcement learning ideas (acting in an environment, gathering feedback) — connecting back to the RL/MDP framework covered in the RLHF context.\n\n20. Progress in LLMs is heavily industry-driven, and many state-of-the-art models are closed-source, which makes independent reproducibility and evaluation difficult; open models (Llama, DeepSeek, Qwen, Mistral) have however substantially closed the gap with closed models. Training these models is extremely energy-intensive: GPT-3 (2020, 175 billion parameters) was estimated to produce around 550 tons of CO2 during training, and newer, larger models consume even more. This raises the broader philosophical question of whether an LLM is genuinely "intelligent" (the strong vs. weak AI debate).\n\nBeyond compute costs, generative models raise concrete risk categories: short-term risks include hallucination (confidently generating false information), susceptibility to prompt injection attacks, and being manipulated into producing toxic content; longer-term risks fall under "alignment" — the worry that increasingly capable AI systems might pursue goals misaligned with human intentions, feeding into broader debates about AI race dynamics and loss of human control. Separately, since generative models are trained on internet-scale data, they can reproduce and amplify societal biases and stereotypes present in that data, and raise unresolved questions about data consent and copyright.\n\nReturn your result as a JSON array of exactly 21 strings, one title per numbered overview above, in the same order (index 0 first).\n\nWhen finished, write your final result to a file named .fatass-result.json in the current directory, as a JSON array.'
